// Interview problems: advanced (conceptual/symbolic), Young & Freedman. Fields: id, title, q (statement), figs, figDesc, sol (examiner-only reference), answers.
(window.EXAMPLES_ADV=window.EXAMPLES_ADV||{}).phys=[{"id":"2.1","title":"Why a jumper seems to hang in the air","q":"An athlete jumps vertically and reaches a maximum height h above the floor. Treat him as a particle in free fall with acceleration g and no air resistance. (a) Derive, in terms of h and g only, the time he spends above height h/2 and the time he takes to rise from the floor to h/2. (b) Find the ratio of these two times and explain how it accounts for the impression that he hangs in the air near the top of the jump.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Choose the apex as the reference: the motion is symmetric, so with $t\u0027$ measured from the apex, height below the top is $\\tfrac12 g t\u0027^2$ (the fall from rest).\n2. Time from apex to the top of the flight from the floor: $T_{up} = \\sqrt{2h/g}$.\n3. The athlete is above $h/2$ while he is within $h/2$ of the apex: $\\tfrac12 g t\u0027^2 = h/2 \\Rightarrow t\u0027 = \\sqrt{h/g}$. So the time above $h/2$ is $2\\sqrt{h/g}$ (up and down).\n4. Time from floor to $h/2$: $T_{up} - \\sqrt{h/g} = (\\sqrt2 - 1)\\sqrt{h/g}$.\n5. Ratio $= 2/(\\sqrt2 - 1) = 2(\\sqrt2 + 1) \\approx 4.83$.\n6. Interpretation: the speed is proportional to the time from the apex, so near the top he moves slowly; he spends $2\\sqrt{h/g}$ out of a total $2\\sqrt{2h/g}$, i.e. $1/\\sqrt2 \\approx 71\\%$ of the flight time, in the upper half of the height. Probe: compute the fraction of time spent in the top quarter of the height ($\\approx 50\\%$), and check that the total time is $2\\sqrt{2h/g}$.","answers":["Time above h/2: 2√(h/g); time from floor to h/2: (√2 − 1)√(h/g)","Ratio = 2/(√2 − 1) = 2(√2 + 1) ≈ 4.83","Speed ∝ time from the apex, so he moves slowly in the top half and spends about 71% of the total time 2√(2h/g) there"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 2.96"},{"id":"2.2","title":"Collision of a thrown-up ball and a dropped ball","q":"A ball is thrown straight up from the ground with speed v₀. At the same instant a second ball is dropped from rest from height H directly above the launch point. Ignore air resistance; g is the gravitational acceleration. (a) Without solving two quadratic equations, explain why the time at which the balls meet is independent of g, and find it. (b) Find H in terms of v₀ and g such that the balls meet at the instant the first ball is at the top of its flight.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Both balls have the same acceleration $-g$, so in the frame of either ball the other moves at constant relative velocity: no acceleration appears in the relative motion.\n2. At $t=0$ the separation is $H$ and the first ball approaches the second at relative speed $v_0$ (first ball $+v_0$, second ball at rest). Separation: $H - v_0 t$.\n3. They meet when $H - v_0 t = 0$: $t = H/v_0$, independent of $g$. Check by direct algebra: $v_0t - \\tfrac12gt^2 = H - \\tfrac12gt^2$ gives the same result (the $g$ terms cancel).\n4. (b) The first ball is at its top at $t = v_0/g$. Setting $H/v_0 = v_0/g$ gives $H = v_0^2/g$.\n5. Check: at that moment ball 1 is at height $v_0^2/2g$ and ball 2 has fallen $\\tfrac12 g(v_0/g)^2 = v_0^2/2g$ from $H=v_0^2/g$, so both are at $v_0^2/2g$. For $H \u003e v_0^2/g$ they meet while the first ball is descending; for $H \u003c v_0^2/g$ they meet while it is still rising.\n6. Probe: at the meeting time their velocities differ by exactly $v_0$ (relative velocity is constant), and the meeting happens before the ground only if $t = H/v_0 \u003c 2v_0/g$ i.e. $H \u003c 2v_0^2/g$.","answers":["(a) Same acceleration, so the relative velocity is constant: t = H/v₀, independent of g","(b) H = v₀²/g (both balls at height v₀²/(2g))"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 2.94"},{"id":"2.3","title":"Catching a bus that pulls away","q":"A student runs at constant speed v toward a bus. When she is a distance d from the bus, which is at rest, the bus starts to move away from her with constant acceleration a. (a) Show that she catches the bus only if v² ≥ 2ad, and find the minimum speed vmin, the time and the distance she runs in that case. (b) If v \u003e vmin, the position equations have two solutions: find the time of each and say what the second one means physically and how fast the bus moves at each.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Origin at the student\u0027s starting position, bus ahead: $x_S = vt$, $x_B = d + \\tfrac12 a t^2$.\n2. Meeting: $vt = d + \\tfrac12 a t^2 \\Rightarrow \\tfrac12 a t^2 - vt + d = 0$.\n3. $t = [v \\pm \\sqrt{v^2 - 2ad}]/a$. A real solution requires $v^2 \\ge 2ad$, so $v_{min} = \\sqrt{2ad}$.\n4. At $v_{min}$ the roots coincide: $t = v/a = \\sqrt{2d/a}$. The student runs $x = v^2/a = 2d$, and the bus speed at the meeting is $at = v = v_{min}$: the student only just matches the bus\u0027s speed as she reaches it (the x-t graphs touch tangentially).\n5. For $v \u003e v_{min}$, the first root $t_1 = [v - \\sqrt{v^2-2ad}]/a$ is when she reaches the bus; the bus speed is $at_1 \u003c v$ (she is faster).\n6. The second root $t_2 = [v + \\sqrt{v^2-2ad}]/a$ would be when the accelerating bus overtakes her again if she continued with $v$ constant (she would be passed since the bus speed $at_2 \u003e v$).\n7. Probe: what if she accelerates too? The criterion changes to a relative-acceleration problem. Check dimensions: $v^2/(ad)$ dimensionless.","answers":["Catch condition: v² ≥ 2ad; v_min = √(2ad)","At v_min: t = v/a = √(2d/a), distance run = 2d, bus speed = v_min","For v \u003e v_min, t = [v ∓ √(v² − 2ad)]/a; the first is catching the bus (bus slower), the second is when the faster bus would pass her again (bus speed \u003e v)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 2.97 (symbolic form)"},{"id":"2.4","title":"Two balls thrown and dropped to land together","q":"Ball 1 is thrown straight up with speed v₀ from the edge of a roof of height h above the ground. Ball 2 is dropped from rest from the same point a time τ later. Ignore air resistance; g is the gravitational acceleration. (a) Use the separation between the balls to show that both reach the ground at the same instant only if gτ/2 \u003c v₀ \u003c gτ, and find h in terms of v₀, g and τ. (b) Explain physically what happens at each limit of v₀.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. For $t \\ge \\tau$ both balls are in flight. With height measured up from the roof, $y_1 = v_0 t - \\tfrac12 g t^2$, $y_2 = -\\tfrac12 g (t-\\tau)^2$.\n2. Separation: $y_1 - y_2 = (v_0 - g\\tau)\\,t + \\tfrac12 g\\tau^2$, linear in $t$ (the $t^2$ terms cancel because the accelerations are equal).\n3. Simultaneous landing means the separation is zero at the landing time $t_L$: $t_L = \\dfrac{g\\tau^2}{2(g\\tau - v_0)}$. This requires $v_0 \u003c g\\tau$ for $t_L\u003e0$, and $t_L\u003e\\tau$ (ball 2 released before landing) requires $v_0 \u003e g\\tau/2$.\n4. Height from ball 2\u0027s fall: $h = \\tfrac12 g (t_L - \\tau)^2$. With $t_L - \\tau = \\tau\\,(v_0 - g\\tau/2)/(g\\tau - v_0)$: $h = \\dfrac{g\\tau^2\\,(v_0 - g\\tau/2)^2}{2\\,(g\\tau - v_0)^2}$.\n5. Limits: as $v_0\\to g\\tau/2$, $h\\to 0$: ball 1 returns to the roof (at $t = 2v_0/g = \\tau$) just as ball 2 is released, so they can only land together if the roof is at ground level. As $v_0\\to g\\tau$, $h\\to\\infty$: at $t = 2\\tau$ ball 1 is back at roof level with downward speed $g\\tau$, equal to ball 2\u0027s speed then, so the separation stays constant and ball 2 never catches up. For $v_0 \u003e g\\tau$ ball 1 is always above ball 2 and lands later.\n6. Check: $v_0=6.0$ m/s, $\\tau=1.00$ s gives $h \\approx 0.41$ m.","answers":["Landing together requires gτ/2 \u003c v₀ \u003c gτ","h = g τ² (v₀ − gτ/2)² / [2 (gτ − v₀)²], landing at t = gτ²/[2(gτ − v₀)]","Lower limit: h → 0 (ball 1 returns to the roof as ball 2 is released); upper limit: h → ∞ (ball 1 returns with the same speed ball 2 has, so their separation never closes)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 2.99 (symbolic form)"},{"id":"3.1","title":"Projectile that always moves away from its launch point","q":"A projectile is launched from point P with speed v₀ at angle α above the horizontal. Ignore air resistance; g is the gravitational acceleration. Find the largest launch angle αmax for which the projectile\u0027s distance from P increases at all times until it lands. Explain the geometric meaning of the limiting case.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Distance squared from the launch point: $r^2 = (v_0\\cos\\alpha\\,t)^2 + (v_0\\sin\\alpha\\,t - \\tfrac12 g t^2)^2 = v_0^2t^2 - v_0 g\\sin\\alpha\\,t^3 + \\tfrac14 g^2 t^4$.\n2. The distance never decreases if $d(r^2)/dt \\ge 0$ for all $t\u003e0$: $2v_0^2 t - 3v_0 g\\sin\\alpha\\,t^2 + g^2t^3 \\ge 0$, i.e. $g^2t^2 - 3v_0 g\\sin\\alpha\\,t + 2v_0^2 \\ge 0$ for all $t$.\n3. A quadratic in $t$ with positive leading coefficient is non-negative for all $t$ iff its discriminant is non-positive: $9v_0^2g^2\\sin^2\\alpha - 8g^2v_0^2 \\le 0$, so $\\sin^2\\alpha \\le 8/9$.\n4. $\\sin\\alpha_{max} = 2\\sqrt2/3$, equivalently $\\cos\\alpha_{max} = 1/3$, so $\\alpha_{max} \\approx 70.5^\\circ$.\n5. Geometric meaning: $d(r^2)/dt = 2\\vec r\\cdot\\vec v$. At the limit the velocity is perpendicular to the position vector at one instant ($t = 3v_0\\sin\\alpha/2g$, the double root) and the distance is momentarily stationary; for steeper launch the ball moves back toward $P$ before landing.\n6. Probes: check that $\\alpha = 90^\\circ$ obviously fails (ball returns to $P$); check $\\alpha=0$ is fine; the answer is independent of $v_0$ and $g$ (dimensionless argument).","answers":["α_max = arcsin(2√2/3) = arccos(1/3) ≈ 70.5°, independent of v₀ and g","Limit: r·v = 0 at one instant, i.e. the velocity is momentarily perpendicular to the position vector from P"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 3.88"},{"id":"3.2","title":"Range of a projectile launched along an incline","q":"A projectile is launched from a point on a plane inclined at angle θ above the horizontal, with speed v₀ at angle φ above the incline surface (measured in the vertical plane containing the line of steepest slope, up the slope). It lands on the incline. Ignore air resistance; g is the gravitational acceleration. (a) Find the range R measured along the incline in terms of v₀, g, θ and φ. (b) Find the angle φ that maximizes R, and the maximum range. (c) Show that your results reduce to the standard level-ground results for θ = 0 and state what changes for a downward slope.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Choose axes along the incline ($x\u0027$ up the slope) and perpendicular to it ($y\u0027$). Then $v_{0x\u0027} = v_0\\cos\\varphi$, $v_{0y\u0027} = v_0\\sin\\varphi$, and gravity has components $a_{x\u0027} = -g\\sin\\theta$, $a_{y\u0027} = -g\\cos\\theta$.\n2. Landing on the incline means $y\u0027=0$: $t = 2v_0\\sin\\varphi/(g\\cos\\theta)$.\n3. Range: $R = v_0\\cos\\varphi\\,t - \\tfrac12 g\\sin\\theta\\,t^2 = \\dfrac{2v_0^2\\sin\\varphi}{g\\cos^2\\theta}\\left[\\cos\\varphi\\cos\\theta - \\sin\\varphi\\sin\\theta\\right] = \\dfrac{2v_0^2\\sin\\varphi\\cos(\\theta+\\varphi)}{g\\cos^2\\theta}$.\n4. (b) Use $2\\sin\\varphi\\cos(\\theta+\\varphi) = \\sin(2\\varphi+\\theta) - \\sin\\theta$. It is maximal when $2\\varphi + \\theta = 90^\\circ$, i.e. $\\varphi = 45^\\circ - \\theta/2$ (the launch direction bisects the angle between the incline and the vertical). Then $R_{max} = \\dfrac{v_0^2(1-\\sin\\theta)}{g\\cos^2\\theta} = \\dfrac{v_0^2}{g(1+\\sin\\theta)}$.\n5. (c) For $\\theta=0$: $R = v_0^2\\sin2\\varphi/g$ and $\\varphi = 45^\\circ$, $R_{max} = v_0^2/g$. For a downward slope replace $\\theta\\to-\\theta$: $\\varphi = 45^\\circ + |\\theta|/2$ and $R_{max} = v_0^2/[g(1-\\sin|\\theta|)]$, longer than on level ground.\n6. Check: as $\\theta\\to90^\\circ$, $R_{max}\\to v_0^2/2g$ = max height of a vertical throw.","answers":["R = 2v₀² sinφ cos(θ + φ) / (g cos²θ)","Optimal φ = 45° − θ/2; R_max = v₀² / [g(1 + sinθ)]","θ = 0 gives R = v₀² sin2φ/g, 45°, v₀²/g; downhill: φ = 45° + |θ|/2, R_max = v₀²/[g(1 − sin|θ|)]"],"src":"Bridging Problem, ch. 3 (Young \u0026 Freedman)"},{"id":"3.3","title":"Bottle lost overboard in a river","q":"Two canoeists paddle upstream with constant effort (constant speed c relative to the water). They accidentally drop a bottle, which then drifts with the current. They continue upstream for a time T, reaching a point a distance d (measured relative to the shore) upstream of the point where the bottle was dropped. Then they turn around and paddle downstream with the same effort and catch the bottle at a distance D downstream from the turning point (relative to the shore). Without solving simultaneous equations, find the river speed u in terms of d, D and T, and the canoe speed c in still water. Why is the answer independent of c?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Work in the frame of the water. There the bottle is at rest, and the canoe moves away from it at constant speed $c$ for time $T$, turns around and returns at speed $c$. The return takes the same time $T$, so the bottle is picked up at total time $2T$.\n2. In the shore frame the bottle drifts at the river speed $u$ for the whole time $2T$, so the distance it travels is $2uT$, measured downstream from the drop point.\n3. From the geometry in the shore frame, the pick-up point is $D$ downstream of the turning point, which is $d$ upstream of the drop point. So the bottle\u0027s displacement from the drop point is $D - d$.\n4. Therefore $2uT = D - d$, i.e. $u = (D-d)/(2T)$. With $d = 2.0$ km, $D = 5.0$ km, $T = 1.0$ h: $u = 1.5$ km/h.\n5. Canoe speed: upstream the ground speed was $c - u = d/T$, so $c = d/T + u = (D+d)/(2T)$. Numbers: $c = 3.5$ km/h.\n6. The result is independent of $c$ because the paddling effort cancels in the water frame (equal times out and back); only the current moves the bottle. Probe: if the river stopped ($u=0$), then $D = d$ and the bottle is found at the drop point; ask what $D$ would be if the canoeists turned back immediately ($T\\to0$ gives $D\\to d$ too).","answers":["u = (D − d)/(2T) (1.5 km/h for d = 2.0 km, D = 5.0 km, T = 1.0 h)","c = (D + d)/(2T) (3.5 km/h)","In the water frame the bottle is at rest and the canoe goes out and back at the same speed, so the return takes the same time T; the effort c cancels"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 3.89 (symbolic form)"},{"id":"3.4","title":"Speed change and radius of curvature of a trajectory","q":"A particle moves with velocity v and acceleration a along a curved path. (a) Show that the rate of change of its speed is the component of a along v, d|v|/dt = a·v/|v|, and that the component perpendicular to v changes only the direction of motion. (b) Use this to find the points of a projectile\u0027s trajectory (launched with speed v₀ at angle α₀ above the horizontal, no air resistance, gravitational acceleration g) where the speed is minimum, and find the radius of curvature of the path at the highest point and at launch.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) $v^2 = \\vec v\\cdot\\vec v$, so $d(v^2)/dt = 2\\vec v\\cdot\\vec a$. Then $2v\\,dv/dt = 2\\vec v\\cdot\\vec a$, giving $dv/dt = \\hat v\\cdot\\vec a = a_\\parallel$. The perpendicular component does not appear, so it changes direction only (for circular motion, $a_\\perp = v^2/R$).\n2. (b) For a projectile $\\vec a = -g\\hat\\jmath$, so $a_\\parallel = -g\\,v_y/v$. The speed decreases while $v_y\u003e0$, is stationary at $v_y=0$ (the apex), and increases afterwards, so the minimum speed is at the apex: $v_{min} = v_0\\cos\\alpha_0$.\n3. At the apex the velocity is horizontal and $\\vec a$ is entirely perpendicular: $a_\\perp = g = v^2/\\rho$, so $\\rho_{apex} = v_0^2\\cos^2\\alpha_0/g$.\n4. At launch the angle between $\\vec v$ and the vertical is $90^\\circ - \\alpha_0$, so $a_\\perp = g\\cos\\alpha_0$ and $\\rho_{launch} = v_0^2/(g\\cos\\alpha_0)$.\n5. Checks: for $\\alpha_0 = 0$, $\\rho_{launch} = v_0^2/g$, the same as at the apex; for $\\alpha_0\\to90^\\circ$, $\\rho_{apex}\\to0$ (the path turns sharply at the top) and $\\rho_{launch}\\to\\infty$ (nearly straight line).\n6. Probe: where is the speed equal to $v_0$ again? At the landing point at the same height.","answers":["dv/dt = a·v/|v| = a_∥; a_⊥ changes only the direction","Minimum speed at the apex, v_min = v₀ cosα₀","Radius of curvature at the apex ρ = v₀² cos²α₀ / g; at launch ρ = v₀² / (g cosα₀)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §3.2 (parallel and perpendicular components) with Test Your Understanding 3.2 and §3.4"},{"id":"4.1","title":"Forces between the links of a lifted chain","q":"A chain of N identical links, each of mass m, hangs from a light rope. The rope pulls upward on the top link with a constant force F, so the whole chain accelerates upward. Number the links j = 1 (top) to N (bottom). (a) Find the acceleration of the chain. (b) Find the force that link j exerts on link j + 1 (the link below it) in terms of F, N and j. (c) Name the Newton\u0027s third law pairs involved, and explain why the ranking of these forces does not depend on g or on whether F is larger than Nmg.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Choose the body: the whole chain, mass $Nm$, external forces F (up) and $Nmg$ (down): $F - Nmg = Nma$, so $a = F/(Nm) - g$.\n2. For links $j+1,\\dots,N$ (the part below link j), mass $(N-j)m$, the external forces are the force $T_j$ from link j (up) and their weight: $T_j - (N-j)mg = (N-j)m\\,a$.\n3. Substitute $a + g = F/(Nm)$: $T_j = (N-j)m(a+g) = (N-j)F/N$. The force decreases linearly from $F(N-1)/N$ (between links 1 and 2) to $F/N$ (between links N-1 and N).\n4. Check with $N=3$, $m=0.250$ kg, $F=9.00$ N: $a = 2.20$ m/s², $T_1 = 6.00$ N, $T_2 = 3.00$ N. The rope force on the top link, $F=9.00$ N, is the largest.\n5. Third-law pairs: link $j$ on $j+1$ with link $j+1$ on $j$ (equal and opposite, acting on different bodies, so they appear in different free-body diagrams); rope on top link with top link on rope.\n6. Ranking: $T_j = (N-j)F/N$ is a fixed fraction of F, so the ranking is the same for any $g$ and for $F$ above or below $Nmg$ (when $F = Nmg$, $a=0$ and $T_j = (N-j)mg$, the static tension).","answers":["a = F/(Nm) − g","Force of link j on link j+1: T_j = (N − j)F/N (linear decrease down the chain; independent of g)","Third-law pairs are the mutual forces between adjacent links and between the rope and the top link; each pair acts on different bodies"],"src":"Bridging Problem, ch. 4 (Young \u0026 Freedman), generalized"},{"id":"4.2","title":"Mass driven by a coupled time-dependent force","q":"An object of mass m is at rest in equilibrium at the origin. At t = 0 a force with components Fx = k₁ + k₂ y and Fy = k₃ t is applied, where k₁, k₂, k₃ are constants and y is the object\u0027s y-coordinate. Find the position and velocity vectors as functions of time. State which component you solve first and why.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Newton\u0027s second law by component: $m a_x = k_1 + k_2 y$, $m a_y = k_3 t$.\n2. Solve y first: the y-equation does not involve x, and $F_x$ depends on $y$, so once $y(t)$ is known the x-equation is an ordinary function of time.\n3. Integrate with initial conditions $v_y(0) = y(0) = 0$: $v_y = k_3 t^2/(2m)$ and $y = k_3 t^3/(6m)$.\n4. Then $a_x = k_1/m + k_2k_3 t^3/(6m^2)$. Integrate with $v_x(0)=x(0)=0$: $v_x = k_1 t/m + k_2k_3 t^4/(24m^2)$ and $x = k_1t^2/(2m) + k_2k_3t^5/(120m^2)$.\n5. Vector form: $\\vec v = \\left(\\dfrac{k_1t}{m} + \\dfrac{k_2k_3t^4}{24m^2}\\right)\\hat\\imath + \\dfrac{k_3t^2}{2m}\\hat\\jmath$ and $\\vec r = \\left(\\dfrac{k_1t^2}{2m} + \\dfrac{k_2k_3t^5}{120m^2}\\right)\\hat\\imath + \\dfrac{k_3t^3}{6m}\\hat\\jmath$.\n6. Checks: dimensions (units of $k_2k_3t^5/m^2$ are metres); for $k_2 = 0$ the motions decouple; the constant-acceleration formulas do not apply since the force varies in time.","answers":["v(t) = [k₁t/m + k₂k₃t⁴/(24m²)] i + [k₃t²/(2m)] j","r(t) = [k₁t²/(2m) + k₂k₃t⁵/(120m²)] i + [k₃t³/(6m)] j","Solve y first: Fy depends only on t, and Fx depends on y"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 4.62 (symbolic form)"},{"id":"4.3","title":"Why does the block move but the mason does not?","q":"A mason of mass M stands on a floor with non-skid shoes and pulls horizontally on a light rope tied to a block of mass m. The rope exerts a force of magnitude T on the block, and the rope-block combination pulls back on the mason with an equal force (Newton\u0027s third law), so the mason pulls on the block exactly as hard as the block pulls on him. Explain, using free-body diagrams in symbols, why the block can accelerate while the mason stays at rest. Let the floor exert a friction force of magnitude f_b on the block, and a static friction force of maximum magnitude f_max on the mason. Find the block\u0027s acceleration in terms of T, f_b and m, the largest acceleration that the mason can produce while remaining at rest, and describe what happens if the mason\u0027s shoes are on a very slippery floor.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The third law relates the forces that two different bodies exert on each other (mason-rope, rope-block); it says nothing by itself about motion. Only the forces on a given body enter its second law.\n2. Block (horizontal, massless rope so the rope force is $T$): $T - f_b = m a$, so $a = (T - f_b)/m$; the block accelerates only if $T \u003e f_b$.\n3. Mason (at rest): the rope pulls him toward the block with force $T$; the floor\u0027s static friction balances it, $f = T$. This is possible only if $T \\le f_{max}$.\n4. Largest acceleration with the mason at rest: set $T = f_{max}$: $a_{max} = (f_{max} - f_b)/m$; the block can accelerate only if $f_{max} \u003e f_b$.\n5. The block\u0027s pull on him is equal to his pull on it ($T$ for each), yet the net forces differ because the floor adds a different friction force to each body.\n6. Slippery floor ($f_{max}\\to0$): the mason\u0027s friction cannot balance $T$, so he accelerates toward the block with $a_M = T/M$ while the block moves toward him; with $f_b\\approx0$ both slide, with $a_{block}/a_M = M/m$.\n7. Probes: what if the rope has mass $m_r$? Then the forces at its two ends differ unless it is in equilibrium. Once the block moves at constant speed, $T = f_b$.","answers":["Block: a = (T − f_b)/m; the mason\u0027s static friction f = T balances the rope force if T ≤ f_max","Maximum acceleration with the mason at rest: a_max = (f_max − f_b)/m","The third law relates forces on different bodies; friction from the floor makes the net forces on the two bodies different. On a slippery floor the mason slides too, with a = T/M"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Conceptual Example 4.11 (A Newton\u0027s third law paradox?)"},{"id":"4.4","title":"Ball on a string in an accelerating car, and inertial frames","q":"A small ball of mass m hangs from the ceiling of a car by a light string. The car accelerates horizontally with constant acceleration A relative to the road. (a) In the road frame (inertial) find the steady angle θ of the string from the vertical and the tension T. (b) In the car\u0027s frame the ball is at rest, yet the real forces do not sum to zero. Explain what this says about Newton\u0027s laws in the car\u0027s frame, and what extra term would have to be added there. (c) Show that any frame moving with constant velocity relative to an inertial frame is also inertial.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Road frame: the ball has the car\u0027s acceleration $A$ (horizontal). Forces: tension $T$ along the string, weight $mg$. Horizontal: $T\\sin\\theta = mA$; vertical: $T\\cos\\theta - mg = 0$.\n2. Divide: $\\tan\\theta = A/g$. Square and add: $T = m\\sqrt{g^2 + A^2}$. The string tilts backward relative to the acceleration.\n3. (b) In the car frame the ball has zero acceleration but the real forces ($\\vec T$ and $m\\vec g$) sum to $m\\vec A \\ne 0$. So $\\sum \\vec F = m\\vec a$ and the first law fail in the accelerated frame; the car is not inertial. A \"fictitious force\" $-m\\vec A$ can be added to restore the form, but no body exerts it (no third-law partner), so it is not a real force.\n4. (c) For a body P and frames A (inertial) and B with $\\vec v_{B/A}$ constant, $\\vec v_{P/B} = \\vec v_{P/A} - \\vec v_{B/A}$. Differentiate: $\\vec a_{P/B} = \\vec a_{P/A}$. The accelerations agree, so $\\sum\\vec F = m\\vec a$ holds in B as well, and a body with zero net force has zero acceleration in B.\n5. Checks: $A = 0$ gives $\\theta=0$, $T = mg$; $A\\gg g$ gives $\\theta\\to90^\\circ$. The earth is only approximately inertial because of its rotation and orbital motion.","answers":["tanθ = A/g; T = m√(g² + A²)","In the accelerated car frame the real forces do not sum to zero for a body at rest: Newton\u0027s first/second laws fail; the extra term would be −mA (a fictitious force, no real source)","With constant v(B/A), v(P/B) = v(P/A) − v(B/A) gives a(P/B) = a(P/A): same accelerations, so frame B is inertial"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §4.2 (inertial frames), with Discussion Questions Q4.13 and Q4.16"},{"id":"5.1","title":"Best angle to pull a box at constant speed","q":"A box of weight w is pulled at constant speed along a level floor by a force F at angle θ above the horizontal. The coefficient of kinetic friction is μ. (a) Find F as a function of θ, μ and w. (b) Find the angle that minimizes F and the minimum force, and give a geometric argument that explains the result. (c) Why does pushing at the same angle below the horizontal require a larger force, and what can happen if μ is large?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Constant speed means equilibrium. Horizontal: $F\\cos\\theta = \\mu n$. Vertical: $n + F\\sin\\theta = w$, so $n = w - F\\sin\\theta$ (the pull lifts partly on the box).\n2. Eliminate $n$: $F = \\dfrac{\\mu w}{\\cos\\theta + \\mu\\sin\\theta}$.\n3. $F$ is minimal when the denominator is maximal: $d/d\\theta(\\cos\\theta + \\mu\\sin\\theta) = -\\sin\\theta + \\mu\\cos\\theta = 0$, so $\\tan\\theta = \\mu$ (second derivative negative: a maximum). The maximum is $\\sqrt{1+\\mu^2}$, so $F_{min} = \\dfrac{\\mu w}{\\sqrt{1+\\mu^2}}$.\n4. Geometry: floor\u0027s contact force $\\vec n + \\vec f_k$ makes angle $\\varphi$ with the vertical, with $\\tan\\varphi = \\mu$ (angle of kinetic friction). Three forces in equilibrium ($w$, $F$, contact force) form a closed triangle; $F$ is smallest when it is perpendicular to the contact force, which gives $\\theta = \\varphi$ and $F_{min} = w\\sin\\varphi$.\n5. (c) Pushing downward at $\\theta$ gives $F = \\mu w/(\\cos\\theta - \\mu\\sin\\theta)$: the push increases $n$, hence friction. If $\\tan\\theta \\ge 1/\\mu$ the denominator vanishes or goes negative: no force can move the box (jamming).\n6. Check: $\\mu=0.25$, $w=400$ N: $\\theta = 14.0^\\circ$, $F_{min} = 97$ N versus 100 N for $\\theta = 0$. As $\\mu\\to0$, $F_{min}\\to0$; as $\\mu\\to\\infty$, $\\theta\\to90^\\circ$ and $F\\to w$.","answers":["F = μw / (cosθ + μ sinθ)","Minimum at tan θ = μ (angle of friction); F_min = μw/√(1 + μ²) = w sin φ","Pushing down gives F = μw/(cosθ − μ sinθ), larger because n increases; it can jam if tanθ ≥ 1/μ"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 5.121 (symbolic form)"},{"id":"5.2","title":"Block held at constant height in a rotating cone","q":"A small block of mass m is inside an inverted cone (apex down) that rotates about its vertical axis with period T. The cone walls make angle β with the horizontal, and the coefficient of static friction between block and wall is μs. The block stays at constant height h above the apex. Find the maximum period Tmax and the minimum period Tmin in terms of β, h, μs and g. Explain the direction of friction in each case and check the limit μs = 0.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The block moves in a horizontal circle of radius $R = h/\\tan\\beta$ with acceleration $a = 4\\pi^2R/T^2$ toward the axis. Forces: weight, normal force $n$ (perpendicular to the wall, with components $n\\sin\\beta$ toward the axis and $n\\cos\\beta$ upward) and friction $f$ along the wall.\n2. Slowest rotation ($T_{max}$): the block tends to slide down, so friction acts up the wall with maximum value $\\mu_s n$. Radial: $n\\sin\\beta - \\mu_s n\\cos\\beta = ma$; vertical: $n\\cos\\beta + \\mu_s n\\sin\\beta = mg$.\n3. Dividing: $a = g\\,\\dfrac{\\sin\\beta - \\mu_s\\cos\\beta}{\\cos\\beta + \\mu_s\\sin\\beta}$ and $T_{max} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{h(\\cos\\beta + \\mu_s\\sin\\beta)}{g\\tan\\beta(\\sin\\beta - \\mu_s\\cos\\beta)}}$ (meaningful only if $\\tan\\beta \u003e \\mu_s$; otherwise the block cannot slide down and $T_{max}\\to\\infty$).\n4. Fastest rotation ($T_{min}$): the block tends to slide up, so friction acts down the wall. Radial: $n\\sin\\beta + \\mu_s n\\cos\\beta = ma$; vertical: $n\\cos\\beta - \\mu_s n\\sin\\beta = mg$.\n5. $a = g\\,\\dfrac{\\sin\\beta + \\mu_s\\cos\\beta}{\\cos\\beta - \\mu_s\\sin\\beta}$, giving $T_{min} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{h(\\cos\\beta - \\mu_s\\sin\\beta)}{g\\tan\\beta(\\sin\\beta + \\mu_s\\cos\\beta)}}$.\n6. Checks: $T_{min} \u003c T_{max}$; for $\\mu_s = 0$ both reduce to $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{h/(g\\tan^2\\beta)} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{R/(g\\tan\\beta)}$, the frictionless banked-curve result $\\tan\\beta = v^2/gR$ with $v=2\\pi R/T$.","answers":["T_max = 2π √[ h(cosβ + μs sinβ) / ( g tanβ (sinβ − μs cosβ) ) ] (friction up the wall)","T_min = 2π √[ h(cosβ − μs sinβ) / ( g tanβ (sinβ + μs cosβ) ) ] (friction down the wall)","μs = 0: T = 2π√(R/(g tanβ)) with R = h/tanβ, same as the frictionless banked curve"],"src":"Bridging Problem, ch. 5 (Young \u0026 Freedman)"},{"id":"5.3","title":"Block sliding on a free wedge","q":"A wedge of mass M with an inclined face at angle α to the horizontal rests on a frictionless horizontal table. A block of mass m is placed on the sloping face; there is no friction between block and wedge. The system is released from rest. (a) Find the acceleration of the wedge and the horizontal and vertical components of the block\u0027s acceleration (relative to the table). (b) Check that your answers reduce to the correct results when M is very large and when m is very large. (c) What is the shape of the block\u0027s trajectory as seen by a stationary observer?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Unknowns: normal force $N$ (the same magnitude on block and wedge, Newton\u0027s third law), wedge acceleration $A$ (horizontal, away from the block\u0027s sliding direction), block acceleration relative to the wedge $a_r$ down the slope.\n2. Wedge: only the horizontal component of $N$ acts: $MA = N\\sin\\alpha$.\n3. Block (axes horizontal/vertical): $m(a_r\\cos\\alpha - A) = N\\sin\\alpha$ and $m a_r\\sin\\alpha = mg - N\\cos\\alpha$ (vertical, downward).\n4. Solving: $N = \\dfrac{Mmg\\cos\\alpha}{M + m\\sin^2\\alpha}$, hence $A = \\dfrac{mg\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha}{M + m\\sin^2\\alpha}$ and $a_r = \\dfrac{(M+m)g\\sin\\alpha}{M + m\\sin^2\\alpha}$.\n5. Block (relative to the table): $a_x = \\dfrac{Mg\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha}{M + m\\sin^2\\alpha}$ (horizontal, opposite the wedge) and $a_y = \\dfrac{(M+m)g\\sin^2\\alpha}{M + m\\sin^2\\alpha}$ (downward).\n6. Checks: $M\\to\\infty$: $A\\to0$, $a_x\\to g\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha$, $a_y\\to g\\sin^2\\alpha$, the fixed incline. $m\\to\\infty$: $A\\to g\\cot\\alpha$, $a_x\\to0$, $a_y\\to g$ (free fall). Horizontal momentum: $MA = ma_x$ with the net external horizontal force zero.\n7. (c) Constant accelerations from rest: the path is a straight line, steeper than the incline since $a_y/a_x = (1 + m/M)\\tan\\alpha$.","answers":["Wedge: A = m g sinα cosα / (M + m sin²α)","Block: a_x = M g sinα cosα / (M + m sin²α) (opposite the wedge), a_y = (M + m) g sin²α / (M + m sin²α) downward","M → ∞ gives the fixed incline; m → ∞ gives free fall; the trajectory is a straight line at tan⁻¹[(1 + m/M) tanα] below the horizontal"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 5.122 (symbolic form)"},{"id":"5.4","title":"Falling with quadratic drag: speed versus time","q":"A ball of mass m is dropped from rest and falls with a drag force of magnitude D v² (v is its speed, D a constant). (a) By applying Newton\u0027s second law, deduce the qualitative motion and the terminal speed vt. (b) Derive v(t) and show that it behaves correctly for short and long times. (c) Find the distance fallen as a function of time. A useful identity: ∫dv/(1 − v²/vt²) = vt·artanh(v/vt).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Take downward positive. Newton\u0027s second law: $m\\,dv/dt = mg - Dv^2$. The acceleration starts at $g$ and decreases as $v$ grows; it is zero when $v = v_t$, so $v_t = \\sqrt{mg/D}$ and $dv/dt = g(1 - v^2/v_t^2)$.\n2. Separate variables: $\\dfrac{dv}{1 - v^2/v_t^2} = g\\,dt$. Integrate from $0$: $v_t\\,\\mathrm{artanh}(v/v_t) = gt$.\n3. Therefore $v(t) = v_t\\tanh\\left(\\dfrac{gt}{v_t}\\right)$.\n4. Limits: for $gt \\ll v_t$, $\\tanh x \\approx x$ so $v \\approx gt$ (free fall, drag negligible); for $gt\\gg v_t$, $v\\to v_t$, approaching exponentially: $v_t - v \\approx 2v_t e^{-2gt/v_t}$.\n5. Check the differential equation: $dv/dt = g\\,\\mathrm{sech}^2(gt/v_t) = g(1 - \\tanh^2) = g(1 - v^2/v_t^2)$.\n6. Distance fallen: $y = \\int_0^t v\\,dt\u0027 = \\dfrac{v_t^2}{g}\\ln\\cosh\\left(\\dfrac{gt}{v_t}\\right)$, which is $\\approx \\tfrac12gt^2$ early and $\\approx v_t t - \\dfrac{v_t^2}{g}\\ln 2$ late.\n7. Probes: how would the time to reach $v_t/2$ ($t = 0.55\\,v_t/g$) compare with the free-fall time? What changes for a linear drag law $bv$ (exponential approach, time constant $m/b$)?","answers":["Terminal speed v_t = √(mg/D), where mg = Dv²","v(t) = v_t tanh(gt/v_t); v ≈ gt for small t, v → v_t for large t","Distance fallen y = (v_t²/g) ln cosh(gt/v_t)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 5.124"},{"id":"6.1","title":"Kinetic energy of a spring with mass","q":"A uniform spring of total mass M, relaxed length L and spring constant k has one end fixed. Its free end moves with speed v, and the speed of each point of the spring is assumed to vary linearly with its distance l from the fixed end. (a) Show that the kinetic energy of the spring is (1/6)Mv², not (1/2)Mv². (b) The spring, compressed by X, launches a ball of mass m horizontally on a frictionless surface, and the ball leaves when the spring reaches its relaxed length. Find the ball\u0027s speed, and explain in what sense the spring adds an \u0027effective mass\u0027 to the ball. Check the limit M → 0.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Piece of length $dl$ at distance $l$ has mass $dm=(M/L)\\,dl$ and speed $u=v\\,l/L$.\n2. $K_{spring}=\\int\\tfrac12u^2\\,dm=\\dfrac{M v^2}{2L^3}\\int_0^L l^2\\,dl=\\dfrac{Mv^2}{6}$. The factor 1/3 arises because most of the mass moves slower than the free end.\n3. Energy conservation with no friction: the stored $\\tfrac12kX^2$ becomes the ball\u0027s kinetic energy plus the spring\u0027s, at the instant the spring is relaxed and its free end moves with the ball\u0027s speed $v$: $\\tfrac12kX^2=\\tfrac12mv^2+\\tfrac16Mv^2$.\n4. $v=X\\sqrt{\\dfrac{k}{m+M/3}}$. The spring acts as if a third of its mass rides along with the ball.\n5. Checks for the interviewer: $M\\to0$ gives $v=X\\sqrt{k/m}$ (the ideal result); the ratio of kinetic energies is $K_{spring}/K_{ball}=M/(3m)$; for the book\u0027s values (k = 3200 N/m, X = 2.50 cm, m = 0.053 kg, M = 0.243 kg) the speed drops from 6.14 m/s to 3.86 m/s, and the energies 0.395 J and 0.603 J add to 1.00 J = ½kX².","answers":["K_spring = Mv²/6","v = X√(k/(m + M/3))","effective extra mass = M/3; M → 0 gives v = X√(k/m)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 6.103"},{"id":"6.2","title":"Block falling on a spring that disobeys Hooke\u0027s law","q":"A block of mass m hangs from the end of a vertical spring of negligible mass that does not obey Hooke\u0027s law: when the spring is stretched by x its force has magnitude αx², where α is a positive constant. The block is released from rest with the spring unstretched. Find, in terms of m, g and α: (a) the block\u0027s speed after it has fallen a distance x₁, (b) the rate at which the spring does work on it at that point, (c) the maximum stretch x₂, and (d) whether the block can stay at x₂. Compare with an ideal spring of constant k.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Spring work on the block over a stretch x: $W_s=-\\int_0^x\\alpha x\u0027^2dx\u0027=-\\alpha x^3/3$ (negative, since the force opposes the motion). Gravity does $+mgx$.\n2. (a) Work–energy: $\\tfrac12mv_1^2=mgx_1-\\alpha x_1^3/3$, so $v_1=\\sqrt{2gx_1-\\dfrac{2\\alpha x_1^3}{3m}}$. For small $x_1$ this reduces to free fall, $v=\\sqrt{2gx_1}$.\n3. (b) $P=\\vec F\\cdot\\vec v=-\\alpha x_1^2v_1$, negative: the spring is removing energy from the block.\n4. (c) At maximum stretch $v=0$: $mgx_2=\\alpha x_2^3/3$, so $x_2=\\sqrt{3mg/\\alpha}$.\n5. (d) No: at $x_2$ the spring force is $\\alpha x_2^2=3mg$, upward and three times the weight, so there is a net upward force $2mg$ and the block is pulled back up.\n6. Probe: equilibrium is at $x_{eq}=\\sqrt{mg/\\alpha}$, so $x_2=\\sqrt3\\,x_{eq}$. For a Hooke spring $x_2=2mg/k=2x_{eq}$ (the standard factor 2). The speed is largest at $x_{eq}$ where the net force vanishes.","answers":["(a) v₁ = √(2g x₁ − 2αx₁³/(3m))","(b) P = −α x₁² v₁ (spring removes energy)","(c) x₂ = √(3mg/α)","(d) No; net force there is 2mg upward, equilibrium is at √(mg/α)"],"src":"Bridging Problem, ch. 6 (A Spring That Disobeys Hooke\u0027s Law)"},{"id":"6.3","title":"Box on an icy plank: will it stay at rest?","q":"A box is shoved up a plank inclined at angle α above the horizontal. The plank has more ice near its bottom, so the coefficient of friction increases with distance x up the plank: μ = Ax (A is a positive constant, x = 0 at the bottom; kinetic and static coefficients are equal). The box leaves the bottom with speed v₀. Show that when it first comes to rest it stays at rest if v₀² ≥ 3g sin²α /(A cos α). Explain why the condition is a threshold in v₀.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Going up, the forces along the plank are $mg\\sin\\alpha$ and kinetic friction $\\mu mg\\cos\\alpha=Axmg\\cos\\alpha$, both down the slope. The friction force varies with x, so integrate.\n2. Work–energy from the bottom to the stopping point $x_s$: $-\\int_0^{x_s}mg(\\sin\\alpha+Ax\\cos\\alpha)dx=-\\tfrac12mv_0^2$, i.e. $v_0^2=2g\\sin\\alpha\\,x_s+gA\\cos\\alpha\\,x_s^2$.\n3. The box stays at rest if static friction can hold it: $\\mu mg\\cos\\alpha\\ge mg\\sin\\alpha$, i.e. $Ax_s\\ge\\tan\\alpha$, i.e. $x_s\\ge\\tan\\alpha/A$.\n4. The right side of step 2 increases with $x_s$, so $x_s\\ge\\tan\\alpha/A$ is equivalent to $v_0^2$ being at least the value at $x_s=\\tan\\alpha/A$: $v_0^2\\ge 2g\\sin\\alpha\\tan\\alpha/A+gA\\cos\\alpha\\tan^2\\alpha/A^2=\\dfrac{3g\\sin^2\\alpha}{A\\cos\\alpha}$.\n5. Interpretation: a faster box travels farther up, into the region where ice is thinner (larger μ) so friction is high enough to hold it. Probes: $A\\to\\infty$ makes the threshold vanish (high friction everywhere); as $\\alpha\\to0$ the box always stays at rest; the answer does not depend on m.","answers":["Stopping point satisfies v₀² = 2g x_s sin α + gA x_s² cos α","Stays at rest if A x_s ≥ tan α, which gives v₀² ≥ 3g sin²α/(A cos α)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 6.102"},{"id":"6.4","title":"Who supplies the kinetic energy?","q":"(a) Two iceboats of masses m and 2m start from rest on frictionless ice and are driven the same distance s by the same constant wind force F. Compare their kinetic energies, speeds, momenta and travel times at the finish line. (b) A skater on frictionless skates pushes against a rigid wall and glides away with kinetic energy K. The wall\u0027s force acts on her hands, which do not move. Does the wall do work on her? Where does K come from, and why does the work–energy theorem need care here?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Each boat receives work $W=Fs$ and starts from rest, so both finish with $K=Fs$ (equal).\n2. From $K=\\tfrac12mv^2$: speed $v=\\sqrt{2Fs/m}$, so the lighter boat is faster by $\\sqrt2$.\n3. Momentum $p=\\sqrt{2mK}$, so the heavy boat has $\\sqrt2$ times more momentum; since $p=Ft$ (constant force from rest), its time is also $\\sqrt2$ times larger. Equal energy, different momentum: work is force times distance, impulse is force times time.\n4. (b) The point of application of the wall force does not move, so the wall does zero work on her. A skater is not a point particle: her hands stay fixed while her torso moves away, so one part of her body does work on another (internal muscle forces, powered by chemical energy).\n5. The work–energy theorem $W_{tot}=\\Delta K$ holds for a particle (or rigid body), but for a deformable system the internal forces can do net work, so K can change with zero external work. The wall supplies momentum (impulse), not energy.\n6. Probe: ask what changes if the wall is replaced by a person on skates (both move), and which conserved quantity the external force does change.","answers":["(a) Equal K = Fs; speed ratio v_m/v_2m = √2; momentum ratio p_2m/p_m = √2; time ratio t_2m/t_m = √2","(b) Wall does no work; the kinetic energy comes from internal (chemical) energy via work done by one part of her body on another; the theorem applies cleanly only to point-like or rigid bodies"],"src":"Conceptual Example 6.5 + §6.2 (Work and Kinetic Energy in Composite Systems)"},{"id":"7.1","title":"Proton in an inverse-square minus inverse potential","q":"A proton of mass m moves along the x-axis (x \u003e 0) with potential energy U(x) = α/x² − β/x, where α and β are positive constants. Let x₀ = α/β. (a) Show that U(x) = (α/x₀²)[(x₀/x)² − x₀/x], find U(x₀), and locate the equilibrium point and minimum value of U. (b) The proton is released from rest at x₀: find its speed v(x), where it is fastest, and describe its long-time motion. (c) If it is released instead from x₁ = 3α/β, find the turning points and describe the motion.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Substituting $\\beta=\\alpha/x_0$: $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{x^2}-\\dfrac{\\alpha}{x_0x}=\\dfrac{\\alpha}{x_0^2}\\Big[\\Big(\\dfrac{x_0}{x}\\Big)^2-\\dfrac{x_0}{x}\\Big]$; at $x=x_0$ the bracket is 0, so $U(x_0)=0$.\n2. $F=-dU/dx=2\\alpha/x^3-\\beta/x^2$, zero at $x_{eq}=2\\alpha/\\beta=2x_0$; $U(x_{eq})=-\\beta^2/(4\\alpha)$ is the minimum (stable equilibrium).\n3. Release from rest at $x_0$: $E=U(x_0)=0$ and $F(x_0)=\\beta^3/\\alpha^2\u003e0$, so the proton moves to larger x. Energy conservation: $\\tfrac12mv^2=-U$, so $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{m}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\beta}{x}-\\dfrac{\\alpha}{x^2}\\Big)}$.\n4. The speed is maximum at the minimum of U, $x=2x_0$: $v_{max}=\\beta/\\sqrt{2m\\alpha}$, where the force is zero.\n5. For $x\u003ex_0$, $U\u003c0$ so $K=-U\u003e0$ everywhere; since $U\\to0$ from below as $x\\to\\infty$, $v\\to0$ only asymptotically: the proton escapes to infinity (E = 0 is the escape threshold), slowing down past $2x_0$.\n6. Release at $x_1=3x_0$: $E=U(x_1)=-2\\beta^2/(9\\alpha)\u003c0$, a bound state. Turning points solve $\\alpha u^2-\\beta u+2\\beta^2/(9\\alpha)=0$ with $u=1/x$, so $u=\\beta/(3\\alpha)$ or $2\\beta/(3\\alpha)$: $x=3\\alpha/\\beta$ and $x=3\\alpha/(2\\beta)$.\n7. The proton oscillates between $1.5x_0$ and $3x_0$ about $2x_0$. Probe: energy diagram sign of E decides bound versus unbound.","answers":["U(x₀) = 0; equilibrium at x = 2α/β with U_min = −β²/(4α)","Released at x₀: v = √[(2/m)(β/x − α/x²)], maximum v = β/√(2mα) at x = 2α/β, proton escapes to infinity (E = 0)","Released at 3α/β: E = −2β²/(9α), oscillates between x = 3α/(2β) and x = 3α/β"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 7.87"},{"id":"7.2","title":"Package sliding down an incline onto a spring","q":"A package of mass m is released from rest on an incline of angle θ, a distance d (measured along the incline) above the free end of a light spring of constant k that lies along the incline and is fixed at the bottom. The kinetic friction coefficient is μ (assume it is less than tan θ, so the package does start moving, and that friction is the same in both directions). Find (a) the maximum compression x of the spring, (b) how far short of its starting point the package stops when it rebounds (assume it leaves the spring), and (c) the energy dissipated. Check the limit μ → 0.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Mechanical energy is not conserved (friction), so use $K_1+U_1+W_{other}=K_2+U_2$ along the incline. Let $A=mg(\\sin\\theta-\\mu\\cos\\theta)$ and $B=mg(\\sin\\theta+\\mu\\cos\\theta)$.\n2. (a) Down to maximum compression the path length is $d+x$: gravity\u0027s potential drop minus friction work equals stored spring energy: $A(d+x)=\\tfrac12kx^2$, so $x=\\dfrac{A+\\sqrt{A^2+2kAd}}{k}$.\n3. (b) On the way back up, friction now adds to gravity. If the package rises a total path $L=x+y$ from maximum compression (y above the free end): $\\tfrac12kx^2=B\\,L$. Combining with step 2, $L=A(d+x)/B$.\n4. Shortfall from the start: $(d+x)-L=(d+x)\\Big(1-\\dfrac{A}{B}\\Big)=(d+x)\\dfrac{2\\mu\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta+\\mu\\cos\\theta}$.\n5. (c) Dissipated energy $=\\mu mg\\cos\\theta\\,[(d+x)+L]$, which equals $mg\\sin\\theta\\times$(net drop along the incline), since K = 0 and the spring is relaxed at the end.\n6. Check: μ = 0 gives A = B, shortfall 0, so it returns to its starting point and nothing is dissipated, as for a conservative system. For the book\u0027s data (m = 2.00 kg, θ = 53.1°, d = 4.00 m, k = 120 N/m, μ = 0.200): x = 1.06 m, shortfall 1.32 m, dissipated 20.7 J.\n7. Probe: whether it actually leaves the spring requires $L\u003ex$; and static friction would need to hold at the top.","answers":["(a) x = [A + √(A² + 2kAd)]/k with A = mg(sin θ − μ cos θ)","(b) shortfall = (d + x)·2μ cos θ /(sin θ + μ cos θ)","(c) dissipated = 2μ mg sin θ cos θ (d + x)/(sin θ + μ cos θ); μ → 0 gives full return and zero loss"],"src":"Bridging Problem, ch. 7 (A Spring and Friction on an Incline)"},{"id":"7.3","title":"When is a force pair conservative?","q":"A particle moves in the xy-plane under the force F = a y î + b x ĵ, where a and b are constants. (a) Compute the work around the square loop (0,0) → (L,0) → (L,L) → (0,L) → (0,0). (b) For what relation between a and b is the force conservative? Find U(x, y) in that case and verify F = −∇U. (c) Explain physically why the loop work being nonzero means no potential energy exists.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Use $W=\\int\\vec F\\cdot d\\vec l$ on each leg. Leg 1 (y = 0, dl = dx î): $F_x=ay=0$, so $W_1=0$.\n2. Leg 2 (x = L, dl = dy ĵ): $F_y=bL$, $W_2=bL\\cdot L=bL^2$.\n3. Leg 3 (y = L, x from L to 0): $F_x=aL$, $W_3=\\int_L^0aL\\,dx=-aL^2$. Leg 4 (x = 0): $F_y=0$, $W_4=0$.\n4. Total $W=(b-a)L^2$ (loop work equals the circulation times the area enclosed).\n5. (b) A conservative force requires zero work on every closed path, so $a=b$. Then $U=-axy$: $F_x=-\\partial U/\\partial x=ay$ and $F_y=-\\partial U/\\partial y=ax$, which matches. A general test is $\\partial F_x/\\partial y=\\partial F_y/\\partial x$, i.e. $a=b$.\n6. (c) If U existed, the work would be $U_{start}-U_{end}=0$ around a loop. A nonzero loop work means a particle going round repeatedly gains (or loses) energy each time, which is a path-dependent, non-recoverable exchange, like friction (always negative) or an induced electromotive force (a generator loop).\n7. Probe: for $a\\neq b$ the sign of W flips with the direction of traversal, unlike friction; check Example 7.11 ($a=0$, $b=C$) gives $CL^2$.","answers":["Loop work W = (b − a)L²","Conservative only if a = b; then U = −a x y and F = −∇U","Nonzero loop work contradicts the existence of U, since U₁ − U₂ = 0 for a closed path"],"src":"Example 7.11 + Problems 7.84–7.85 (generalized)"},{"id":"7.4","title":"Small oscillations from an energy diagram","q":"A particle of mass m moves in one dimension under a conservative force with potential energy U(x), which has a local minimum at x₀. (a) Explain why x₀ is a stable equilibrium and why a maximum would be unstable. (b) Show that for small displacements the motion is simple harmonic and find the angular frequency in terms of U″(x₀) and m. (c) Apply this to U = α/x² − β/x (α, β \u003e 0) and give ω in terms of α, β, m. (d) What limits how large the oscillation can be before this approximation fails?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) $F=-dU/dx$; just right of a minimum the slope is positive so $F\u003c0$ (toward $x_0$), just left it is negative so $F\u003e0$: a restoring force. At a maximum the signs reverse, so a displacement grows.\n2. (b) Taylor expand: $U\\approx U(x_0)+\\tfrac12U\u0027\u0027(x_0)(x-x_0)^2$ (the linear term vanishes at equilibrium). This is a spring potential with $k_{eff}=U\u0027\u0027(x_0)\u003e0$, so $m\\ddot{x}=-U\u0027\u0027(x_0)(x-x_0)$ and $\\omega=\\sqrt{U\u0027\u0027(x_0)/m}$.\n3. (c) $U\u0027=-2\\alpha/x^3+\\beta/x^2=0$ gives $x_0=2\\alpha/\\beta$. $U\u0027\u0027=6\\alpha/x^4-2\\beta/x^3$; at $x_0$: $\\dfrac{3\\beta^4}{8\\alpha^3}-\\dfrac{\\beta^4}{4\\alpha^3}=\\dfrac{\\beta^4}{8\\alpha^3}$. So $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\beta^4}{8m\\alpha^3}}=\\dfrac{\\beta^2}{\\sqrt{8m\\alpha^3}}$.\n4. Checks: for $U=\\tfrac12kx^2$, $U\u0027\u0027=k$ gives $\\omega=\\sqrt{k/m}$; units of $\\beta^2/\\sqrt{m\\alpha^3}$: with U in J, $\\alpha$ ~ J·m², $\\beta$ ~ J·m, giving s⁻¹.\n5. (d) The approximation holds while $E-U_{min}$ is small compared with the depth of the well so the cubic and higher terms in the expansion are negligible; the well here is asymmetric (steeper at small x), so large oscillations are anharmonic and the period changes, and for $E\\ge0$ the particle escapes.","answers":["ω = √(U″(x₀)/m), with effective spring constant U″(x₀)","For U = α/x² − β/x: x₀ = 2α/β, U″(x₀) = β⁴/(8α³), ω = β²/√(8mα³)","Stable at U minima (restoring force), unstable at maxima; harmonic only while the oscillation is small compared with the anharmonic scale of the well"],"src":"§7.5 Energy Diagrams + Challenge Problem 7.87 (extension)"},{"id":"8.1","title":"Rocket lifting off against gravity","q":"A rocket of instantaneous mass m ejects fuel backward at constant relative speed v_ex while accelerating vertically upward near the Earth\u0027s surface (g constant, no air resistance). (a) Starting from momentum balance over a short time dt, derive the equation of motion and show how the free-space result is modified. (b) Find the acceleration a in terms of v_ex, m, dm/dt and g. (c) For a constant burn rate over a time t, find v(t) starting from rest, and state the lift-off condition. (d) For v_ex = 2400 m/s, burning 1/120 of m₀ per second for 90 s down to m₀/4, evaluate a at t = 0 and v at 90 s.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. System: rocket plus the fuel ejected in $dt$. The external force is gravity, so $dP=-mg\\,dt$ (instead of zero).\n2. Momentum at $t$: $P_1=mv$. At $t+dt$: rocket $(m+dm)(v+dv)$ with $dm\u003c0$, exhaust of mass $-dm$ at velocity $v-v_{ex}$: $P_2=(m+dm)(v+dv)-dm\\,(v-v_{ex})$.\n3. $P_2-P_1=m\\,dv+v_{ex}\\,dm$ (dropping $dm\\,dv$). Setting this equal to $-mg\\,dt$ gives $m\\,dv=-v_{ex}\\,dm-mg\\,dt$.\n4. (b) $a=\\dfrac{dv}{dt}=-\\dfrac{v_{ex}}{m}\\dfrac{dm}{dt}-g$: thrust per unit mass minus g. Lift-off requires the thrust $v_{ex}|dm/dt|$ to exceed the weight $m_0g$ (until then the launch pad supplies a normal force and $a=0$).\n5. (c) Integrate: $v=v_{ex}\\ln\\dfrac{m_0}{m}-gt$. The gravity loss is $gt$, which is why long, slow burns waste delta-v.\n6. (d) $a_0=2400/120-9.8=20-9.8=10.2$ m/s²; $v=2400\\ln4-(9.8)(90)=3327-882=2445$ m/s.\n7. Probes: thrust-to-weight ratio $=20/9.8\\approx2.0$; $g\\to0$ recovers the free-space result; high thrust (short burn) reduces gravity loss.","answers":["m dv/dt = −v_ex dm/dt − mg","a = −(v_ex/m)(dm/dt) − g; lift-off needs v_ex|dm/dt| \u003e m₀g","v = v_ex ln(m₀/m) − gt","a₀ = 10.2 m/s², v(90 s) ≈ 2.45×10³ m/s"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 8.112"},{"id":"8.2","title":"Raindrop gaining mass as it falls","q":"A raindrop falls from rest through a cloud of tiny stationary droplets that stick to it. Its mass grows with the distance fallen: m = kx (k constant), so dm/dt = k v. The only external force is gravity, F = mg, and the correct equation is F = dp/dt = m dv/dt + v dm/dt. (a) Show that the drop has constant acceleration and find it. (b) Find v(x), and the fraction of the gravitational work that appears as the drop\u0027s kinetic energy. (c) Explain physically why the acceleration is less than g and where the rest of the energy goes.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Substitute $m=kx$ and $dm/dt=kv$: $kx\\,g=kx\\,\\dfrac{dv}{dt}+kv^2$, i.e. $xg=x\\,\\dfrac{dv}{dt}+v^2$.\n2. (a) Try $v=at$, so $x=\\tfrac12at^2$ (starting from rest at $x=0$). Then $\\tfrac12at^2g=\\tfrac12at^2\\,a+a^2t^2=\\tfrac32a^2t^2$, hence $a=g/3$, independent of k.\n3. (b) $v^2=2ax=\\tfrac23gx$. Work by gravity $=mgx=kgx^2$; kinetic energy $=\\tfrac12mv^2=\\tfrac12(kx)(\\tfrac23gx)=\\tfrac13kgx^2$. So exactly one third of the work becomes kinetic energy.\n4. (c) The drop must accelerate each captured droplet from rest to its own speed; the collisions are completely inelastic, so momentum is shared but mechanical energy is dissipated (two thirds of the gravitational work ends up as internal energy). Because the force must also accelerate new mass, $dv/dt\u003cg$.\n5. Checks: no accretion ($k\\to0$ in the momentum term) would give g; the result $g/3$ has no k dependence. For the book\u0027s numbers (k = 2.00 g/m, t = 3.00 s) the distance fallen is $gt^2/6=14.7$ m and the mass is 29.4 g.\n6. Probe: using $F=m\\,dv/dt$ alone would be wrong for variable mass; the $v\\,dm/dt$ term is the momentum of the accreted mass.","answers":["a = g/3 (constant, independent of k)","v² = (2/3) g x; 1/3 of the work done by gravity becomes kinetic energy","The rest (2/3) is dissipated in the inelastic accretion of droplets; m dv/dt is only part of F = dp/dt"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 8.114"},{"id":"8.3","title":"Energy transfer in head-on elastic collisions","q":"A body A of mass m_A moves with speed v along the x-axis and collides head-on and elastically with a stationary body B of mass m_B. (a) Using conservation of momentum and the reversal of relative velocity, derive v_A2 and v_B2. (b) Show that the fraction of A\u0027s kinetic energy transferred to B is 4m_A m_B/(m_A + m_B)². For what mass ratio is it largest? (c) Why does a nuclear reactor use light nuclei (like hydrogen in water) as moderators to slow neutrons, and what is the speed factor and energy retained after one head-on collision of a neutron with carbon (m_B = 12 m_A)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Momentum: $m_Av=m_Av_{A2}+m_Bv_{B2}$. Elastic: relative velocity reverses, $v_{B2}-v_{A2}=v$.\n2. Substitute $v_{B2}=v+v_{A2}$: $m_Av=m_Av_{A2}+m_B(v+v_{A2})$, so $v_{A2}=\\dfrac{m_A-m_B}{m_A+m_B}v$ and $v_{B2}=\\dfrac{2m_A}{m_A+m_B}v$.\n3. (b) $\\dfrac{K_B}{K_A}=\\dfrac{\\tfrac12m_Bv_{B2}^2}{\\tfrac12m_Av^2}=\\dfrac{4m_Am_B}{(m_A+m_B)^2}$. This is symmetric in the masses and equals 1 only when $m_A=m_B$ (A stops dead); it falls toward 0 when the masses are very different. Check: $K_A$ after plus $K_B$ equals the original K.\n4. (c) A moderator should take the neutron\u0027s energy in as few collisions as possible, so its nuclei should have mass close to the neutron\u0027s (a head-on hit on hydrogen can stop it). For carbon, $m_B=12m_A$: $v_{A2}=-\\tfrac{11}{13}v$, so the speed factor is $11/13=0.85$ (neutron bounces back) and it keeps $(11/13)^2=0.72$ of its energy; fraction transferred $48/169=0.28$.\n5. Limits to probe: $m_A\\ll m_B$ gives $v_{A2}\\approx-v$ and almost no energy transfer (Ping-Pong ball on a bowling ball); $m_A\\gg m_B$ gives $v_{B2}\\approx2v$ with small energy transfer ($4m_B/m_A$).\n6. Real moderators also require low neutron absorption; water absorbs some neutrons but also serves as coolant.","answers":["v_A2 = (m_A − m_B)v/(m_A + m_B), v_B2 = 2m_A v/(m_A + m_B)","Fraction transferred = 4m_A m_B/(m_A + m_B)², maximum (=1) for m_A = m_B","Light nuclei remove the most energy per collision; with carbon the neutron keeps 11/13 of its speed (≈72% of its energy)"],"src":"§8.4 + Example 8.11 + Test Your Understanding 8.4"},{"id":"8.4","title":"Center of mass with no external force","q":"(a) Two skaters of masses M₁ and M₂ start from rest on frictionless ice a distance D apart and pull themselves together along a light rope. Where do they meet, and why does it not depend on how hard each pulls? (b) A shell fired from level ground explodes at the top of its trajectory (height h, horizontal velocity v₀) into two equal-mass fragments. One fragment is momentarily at rest right after the explosion and falls straight down. Where does the other land, relative to the explosion point, and why? Take g constant and no air resistance.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) The net external horizontal force on the system is zero and the initial total momentum is zero, so $\\vec v_{cm}=0$ at all times: the center of mass stays fixed. They must meet at the center of mass, at a distance $D\\,M_2/(M_1+M_2)$ from skater 1\u0027s starting point (the lighter skater travels farther). Pulling harder changes only how quickly they move (the rope tension is an internal force pair of equal magnitude).\n2. Check: displacements are in inverse ratio of masses, $M_1\\Delta x_1=M_2\\Delta x_2$; for 90.0 kg and 60.0 kg, a 6.0 m move by the heavier is 9.0 m for the lighter (book values).\n3. (b) The explosion forces are internal; the only external force is gravity, so the center of mass keeps following the original parabola until a fragment lands. Right after the explosion both fragments have zero vertical velocity, so both fall in the same time $t=\\sqrt{2h/g}$.\n4. Momentum just after: $m v_0=\\tfrac m2(0)+\\tfrac m2v_2$, so $v_2=2v_0$ (kinetic energy increased: the explosion released chemical energy).\n5. Center of mass at landing time: $x_{cm}=v_0t$ from the explosion point. Fragment 1 lands at 0, so $\\tfrac12(0+x_2)=v_0t$ gives $x_2=2v_0\\sqrt{2h/g}$, consistent with $x_2=v_2t$.\n6. Probes: the cm is not on the ground path of either fragment; once one fragment hits the ground the ground exerts an external force and the center of mass of the remaining system no longer follows the parabola. Momentum is conserved even though kinetic energy is not.","answers":["(a) They meet at the center of mass, D·M₂/(M₁ + M₂) from skater 1\u0027s start, independent of the pulling force","(b) The second fragment lands 2v₀√(2h/g) horizontally from the explosion point: the center of mass keeps following the original parabola; momentum is conserved while K increases"],"src":"§8.5 Motion of the Center of Mass + Example 8.14 + Test Your Understanding 8.5"},{"id":"9.1","title":"Spinning a CD at constant linear scanning speed","q":"On a compact disc the track is a spiral r(θ) = r₀ + bθ (θ = 0 at the inner radius r₀, b a positive constant) that is scanned at a constant linear speed v. (a) Find the angular speed ω as a function of the track radius r. (b) Find the angular acceleration α in terms of v, b and r, and say whether it is constant, and what sign it has. (c) Find r as a function of time, and the playing time to scan from r₀ to r₁. (d) What does b represent physically?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Constant linear speed of the point being read: v = rω, so ω = v/r (the disc must slow down as the head moves outward).\n2. Differentiate: α = dω/dt = −(v/r²)(dr/dt). Along the spiral dr/dt = b dθ/dt = bω = bv/r.\n3. Hence α = −b v²/r³. It is negative (the disc slows down), not constant, and its magnitude falls as 1/r³, being largest at the inner radius.\n4. From r dr/dt = bv: r² = r₀² + 2bvt, so r(t) = √(r₀² + 2bvt) and ω(t) = v/√(r₀² + 2bvt).\n5. Playing time T = (r₁² − r₀²)/(2bv).\n6. Each revolution increases r by 2πb, so 2πb is the track pitch (spacing between neighboring turns); b = pitch/2π.\n7. Probes: constant-α kinematics formulas do not apply here; check units of b (length per radian); at large r, ω and α both go to zero as the spiral lengthens.","answers":["ω = v/r","α = −b v²/r³ (negative, non-constant, ∝ 1/r³)","r(t) = √(r₀² + 2bvt); T = (r₁² − r₀²)/(2bv)","2πb is the track pitch"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 9.101 + Example 9.5/Test Your Understanding 9.3"},{"id":"9.2","title":"Moment of inertia of a uniform cone","q":"A uniform solid cone has mass M, base radius R and altitude h. Derive its moment of inertia about its axis of symmetry in terms of M and R, and explain, by comparison with a solid cylinder and a solid sphere of the same mass and radius, why the numerical coefficient is what it is. Does h appear in the answer?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Slice the cone into thin discs perpendicular to the axis, at distance x from the apex. Disc radius r = Rx/h, thickness dx, dm = ρπr²dx.\n2. Disc about its axis: dI = ½ r² dm = ½ρπ r⁴ dx = ½ρπ (R/h)⁴ x⁴ dx.\n3. Integrate from 0 to h: I = ½ρπ(R/h)⁴ · h⁵/5 = ρπR⁴h/10.\n4. Mass: M = ρ·(⅓πR²h), so ρπR²h = 3M.\n5. Thus I = (3M/10) R² = (3/10)MR². The height h cancels; only the radial distribution of mass about the axis matters.\n6. Comparison: cylinder ½MR² (0.5) \u003e sphere (2/5) MR² = 0.4 \u003e cone 0.3 MR². The cone has more of its mass concentrated near the axis (the apex region contributes almost nothing), so the coefficient is smallest.\n7. Checks: dimensions mass·length²; as R → 0 at fixed M, I → 0.","answers":["I = (3/10)MR²","h cancels out; coefficient 0.3 \u003c 0.4 (sphere) \u003c 0.5 (cylinder) because the cone\u0027s mass lies closer to the axis"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 9.100"},{"id":"9.3","title":"Sphere whose density falls off with radius","q":"A sphere of radius R has density that decreases linearly from ρ₀ at the center to zero at the surface: ρ(r) = ρ₀(1 − r/R). (a) Find its total mass M. (b) Find its moment of inertia about a diameter in terms of M and R. (c) Explain, without integrating, why the coefficient must be less than 2/5, and what would happen if ρ increased with r.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Use thin spherical shells: radius r, thickness dr, dm = ρ(r)4πr²dr.\n2. (a) M = 4πρ₀∫₀^R (r² − r³/R) dr = 4πρ₀(R³/3 − R³/4) = πρ₀R³/3.\n3. (b) A thin shell has I = (2/3) r² dm. So I = (2/3)·4πρ₀∫₀^R (r⁴ − r⁵/R) dr = (8πρ₀/3)(R⁵/5 − R⁵/6) = 4πρ₀R⁵/45.\n4. Divide: I/(MR²) = (4π/45)/(π/3) = 12/45 = 4/15. So I = (4/15)MR² ≈ 0.267 MR².\n5. (c) A uniform sphere gives 2/5 = 0.4. With the mass concentrated toward the center (small r), each mass element contributes less r², so I \u003c (2/5)MR². Conversely, if density increased outward I would exceed (2/5)MR²; the limit of all mass in a thin shell is (2/3)MR².\n6. Checks: 4/15 lies between 0 (point mass at center) and 2/3 (shell).","answers":["M = πρ₀R³/3","I = 4πρ₀R⁵/45 = (4/15)MR²","coefficient \u003c 2/5 because the mass is weighted toward the axis; it would be \u003e 2/5 if density increased outward (shell limit 2/3)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 9.99 (symbolic variant) + §9.6 Fig. 9.21"},{"id":"9.4","title":"Why the tip of a falling rod beats free fall","q":"A uniform thin rod of mass M and length L is pivoted without friction at one end and released from rest in a horizontal position. (a) Find its angular speed ω when it passes the vertical. (b) Find the speed of the free end then, and compare it with the speed √(2gL) of a particle that has fallen the same height L. (c) At what distance x from the pivot does a point of the rod have exactly the free-fall speed for its own drop at that instant, and what does this say about where the extra energy comes from?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Only gravity does work and the pivot does none, so mechanical energy is conserved. For an extended body U = Mgy_cm: the cm drops L/2.\n2. K = ½Iω² with I = ⅓ML² about the end. Then Mg(L/2) = ½(⅓ML²)ω², so ω = √(3g/L).\n3. Tip speed v = ωL = √(3gL) \u003e √(2gL): the tip is about 22% faster than a freely falling particle.\n4. A point at distance x drops by x, so its free-fall speed is √(2gx); the rod\u0027s point moves at ωx = x√(3g/L). Equal when 3x²g/L = 2gx, i.e. x = 2L/3. Points beyond 2L/3 outrun free fall (they are pulled by the rest of the rod).\n5. Interpretation: the rod is rigid, so internal forces tie the motion of all parts together. Parts inside 2L/3 are slower than free fall (held back by the pivot-end), and parts outside 2L/3 are pushed along by the inner parts, so they exceed free fall. Total energy is still only the lost potential energy MgL/2; it is merely redistributed toward the outer mass.\n6. Probe: at release α = (MgL/2)/(ML²/3) = 3g/2L, so the tip\u0027s initial acceleration is 3g/2 \u003e g, the same effect; a point at 2L/3 has acceleration exactly g (the center of percussion for the pivot).","answers":["ω = √(3g/L)","v_tip = √(3gL) \u003e √(2gL)","x = 2L/3; rigid-body internal forces drive the outer part faster than free fall while the total kinetic energy is fixed by MgL/2"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §9.4 (Eq. 9.18, Example 9.8) with Discussion Question style reasoning"},{"id":"10.1","title":"Where to hit a free rod so one end stays at rest","q":"A uniform rod of mass M and length L lies on a frictionless horizontal surface. It is struck by a short horizontal impulse J perpendicular to the rod at a distance x from end A. (a) Find x such that end A has zero velocity immediately after the impact. (b) Explain physically why this point is the \u0027sweet spot\u0027 of a bat or a hammer, and what the same point means if the rod is instead hinged at A.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Translation of the cm: M v_cm = J, so v_cm = J/M.\n2. Rotation about the cm: the impulse acts at distance d = x − L/2 from the cm, so the angular impulse J d = I_cm ω with I_cm = ML²/12, giving ω = 12 J d/(ML²).\n3. End A is at distance L/2 from the cm on the side opposite the impulse (for x \u003e L/2), so v_A = v_cm − ω(L/2) = J/M − 6 J d/(ML).\n4. Set v_A = 0: 1 = 6d/L, so d = L/6 and x = L/2 + L/6 = 2L/3 from A (the center of percussion).\n5. Physical meaning: the translation and rotation contributions cancel at A, so a hand there feels no impulsive force (no \u0027sting\u0027). Equivalently, if the rod were hinged at A, an impulse at 2L/3 requires no impulsive reaction force at the hinge: angular momentum about A gives J x = (ML²/3) ω, and v_cm = ω L/2 = (3J x/2ML) ... which equals J/M exactly when x = 2L/3.\n6. Probes: x = L/2 gives pure translation (v_A = J/M); x \u003e 2L/3 makes A move backward; the result 2L/3 equals I_end/(M L/2) = (ML²/3)/(ML/2), the \u0027equivalent simple pendulum\u0027 length.","answers":["x = 2L/3 measured from end A (center of percussion)","At this point v_cm = ω L/2, so rotation about A needs no impulsive pivot force: no sting for a hand at A"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 10.99 (center of percussion), symbolic version"},{"id":"10.2","title":"Ball rolling down a pair of rails","q":"A uniform solid ball of radius R and mass M rolls without slipping on two parallel inclined rails whose contact points with the ball are a horizontal distance d apart (d \u003c 2R). It starts from rest and descends a vertical height h. (a) Show that the speed of its center satisfies v = ωρ with ρ = √(R² − d²/4), where ω is its angular speed. (b) Find v in terms of g, h, R and d. (c) Discuss the limits d → 0 and d → 2R, and compare with rolling on a single ramp.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Rolling without slipping means the contact points are instantaneously at rest. The ball rotates about a horizontal axis through its center perpendicular to the direction of motion; the contact points lie at perpendicular distance ρ from this axis where ρ² = R² − (d/2)² (they sit at horizontal offset ±d/2 on the sphere of radius R).\n2. Zero velocity of the contact points: v_cm = ωρ.\n3. Energy (friction on the rails does no work): Mgh = ½ M v² + ½ I ω² with I = (2/5)MR² and ω = v/ρ.\n4. Mgh = ½ M v² [1 + (2/5) R²/ρ²], so v² = 2gh / [1 + (2/5)R²/(R² − d²/4)] = 10 g h / [5 + 2/(1 − d²/4R²)].\n5. v = √{10 g h / [5 + 2/(1 − d²/(4R²))]}.\n6. Limits: d → 0 gives ρ = R and v = √(10gh/7), the ordinary rolling-ball result. d → 2R gives ρ → 0, so a given v needs huge ω: all energy goes to rotation and v → 0. The rail version is always slower than the ramp version because more of the released energy is stored in rotation (effective rolling radius is smaller).\n7. Check dimension: gh is a speed squared.","answers":["v_cm = ωρ with ρ = √(R² − d²/4)","v = √{10gh/[5 + 2/(1 − d²/4R²)]}","d → 0: √(10gh/7); d → 2R: v → 0; slower than on a ramp because more energy goes to rotation"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 10.100"},{"id":"10.3","title":"Spinning cylinder set on a rough floor","q":"A uniform solid cylinder of mass M and radius R is set down on a horizontal floor with its center at rest and an initial angular speed ω₀ about its axis. The coefficient of kinetic friction with the floor is μ_k. (a) Find the speed v_f and angular speed at which slipping stops. (b) Find the time and the distance the cylinder moves before it rolls without slipping. (c) Show that angular momentum about a point on the floor is conserved and use it to check part (a). (d) Find the fraction of the initial kinetic energy that is dissipated.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. While slipping, kinetic friction f = μ_k M g acts on the cylinder, giving a_cm = μ_k g (speeding it up) and a torque fR = ½MR² α, so the spin decreases at rate α = 2μ_k g/R.\n2. v(t) = μ_k g t and ω(t) = ω₀ − 2μ_k g t/R. Rolling starts when v = Rω: μ_k g t = Rω₀ − 2μ_k g t, so t = Rω₀/(3μ_k g).\n3. Then v_f = Rω₀/3 and ω_f = ω₀/3. Distance: d = ½ a t² = R²ω₀²/(18 μ_k g).\n4. Angular momentum about a floor point on the line of motion: friction and the normal force pass through or are collinear with that point, and the weight is balanced by the normal force, so there is no net torque. Initial L = ½MR²ω₀; final L = (M v_f R + ½MR² ω_f) = MR²ω₀/3 + MR²ω₀/6 = ½MR²ω₀. ✓ (This gives v_f directly.)\n5. Energy: K_i = ½(½MR²)ω₀² = ¼MR²ω₀²; K_f = ½M v_f² + ½(½MR²)ω_f² = MR²ω₀²(1/18 + 1/36) = MR²ω₀²/12. Loss = MR²ω₀²/6, i.e. two thirds of K_i; it equals the work of kinetic friction (negative on the rotation, positive on the translation, net negative since there is relative sliding).\n6. Probes: the result is independent of μ_k except in time and distance; μ_k → 0 means rolling never starts.","answers":["v_f = Rω₀/3, ω_f = ω₀/3","t = Rω₀/(3μ_k g); d = R²ω₀²/(18 μ_k g)","Angular momentum about a floor point is conserved: ½MR²ω₀ = (3/2)MR²ω_f","Fraction lost = 2/3 of the initial kinetic energy"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 10.101 (cf., symbolic) + §10.3, §10.6"},{"id":"10.4","title":"Why a spinning wheel on a pivot does not fall","q":"A bicycle wheel (all its mass m on a rim of radius R) spins at angular speed ω about a horizontal axle. The axle is supported at one end by a frictionless pivot, with the wheel\u0027s center a distance r from the pivot. (a) Explain, using angular momentum, why the axle precesses instead of falling, and find the precession angular speed Ω for slow precession. (b) What happens to Ω if the wheel\u0027s mass is doubled with ω, R and r fixed, and if the spin slows by friction? (c) State the condition for the formula to be valid and the force the pivot must supply.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Take torques about the pivot. The weight mg acts at the cm and gives a torque τ = mg r, horizontal and perpendicular to the axle. The spin angular momentum is L = Iω = mR²ω along the axle.\n2. dL = τ dt is perpendicular to L: it changes the direction of L (and the axle) but not its magnitude, so the axle swings horizontally instead of falling.\n3. The axle turns through dφ = dL/L in dt, so Ω = τ/L = mg r/(mR²ω) = g r/(R²ω).\n4. Doubling m leaves Ω unchanged (m cancels: the weight and the inertia double together). If friction reduces ω, Ω grows as 1/ω; at large Ω the assumption fails and the wheel nutates and eventually falls.\n5. Validity: L must be essentially the spin angular momentum, i.e. Ω ≪ ω, which requires ω² ≫ g r/R².\n6. Forces: the vertical component of the pivot force must equal mg (no vertical acceleration of the cm), and the horizontal component must supply the centripetal force m Ω² r for the circular motion of the cm.\n7. Probes: reversing the spin direction reverses the precession direction; with ω = 0 the formula gives Ω → ∞, i.e. the wheel simply falls.","answers":["Ω = τ/L = g r/(R²ω), independent of m","Doubling m: no change; slower spin: Ω increases ∝ 1/ω, eventually nutation","Valid for Ω ≪ ω; pivot supplies vertical force mg and horizontal force mΩ²r"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §10.7, Test Your Understanding 10.7 and Problem 10.102 (symbolic)"},{"id":"11.1","title":"Boom held by a cable: which cable angle is best","q":"A horizontal boom of length L and weight W_b (not necessarily uniform) is pivoted at its left end by a frictionless pin and held by a cable attached to its right end and making angle θ with the horizontal. A crate of weight w hangs from an unspecified point of the boom. (a) Show that the cable tension is the same for angles θ and 180° − θ, and that the horizontal force of the pivot on the boom then has equal magnitude but opposite direction. (b) Show that the cable cannot be horizontal. (c) Find the cable angle that minimizes the tension, and the direction of the pivot force in that case.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Take torques about the pivot, which removes the pivot force. All weights together give a clockwise torque M = w x + W_b x_b (x = position of the crate, x_b = the boom\u0027s cg), a fixed number independent of the cable.\n2. The cable acts at the right end (distance L) and only its vertical component T sin θ has a lever arm L: T L sin θ = M, so T = M/(L sin θ).\n3. (a) sin θ = sin(180° − θ), hence the same T for both angles. Horizontal balance: pivot H = T cos θ in magnitude; cos(180° − θ) = −cos θ, so the pivot\u0027s horizontal component is equal in size and opposite in direction.\n4. (b) For θ = 0 we would need T L sin θ = M \u003e 0 with sin θ = 0, impossible (the tension would have to be infinite). A horizontal cable has no lever arm about the pivot, so it cannot balance the weights\u0027 torque.\n5. (c) T = M/(L sin θ) is smallest when sin θ = 1, θ = 90°, giving T_min = M/L.\n6. With the cable vertical, cos θ = 0, so the horizontal force on the boom from the pivot is zero: the pivot force is purely vertical, with value w + W_b − T = w + W_b − M/L.\n7. Probes: the tension blows up as θ → 0 (long lever arm lost) and drops to a minimum for a vertical cable; a \u0027sideways\u0027 cable is wasteful. Check the vertical force balance: V + T sin θ = w + W_b.","answers":["T = M/(L sin θ), M = total weight torque about the pivot; same for θ and 180° − θ","Horizontal cable impossible (zero lever arm, infinite T needed)","T is minimum for a vertical cable, T_min = M/L, and then the pivot force is purely vertical"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 11.84"},{"id":"11.2","title":"Rod hung from two wires of different stiffness","q":"A light rod of length L is suspended horizontally by two vertical wires A and B of the same length attached at its ends. The wires have cross-sectional areas A_A and A_B and Young\u0027s moduli Y_A and Y_B. A weight w is hung from a point at distance x from the end where A is attached. (a) Find x so that the two wires carry equal stress. (b) Find x so that the two wires have equal strain. (c) Is the rod horizontal in case (a)? In case (b)? Explain what principle, beyond force and torque balance, decides this.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Statics of the (massless) rod: torques about the point where wire B attaches give T_A L = w(L − x), so T_A = w(L − x)/L; torques about A give T_B = w x/L. Check: T_A + T_B = w.\n2. (a) Equal stress means T_A/A_A = T_B/A_B: (L − x)/A_A = x/A_B, so x = L A_B/(A_A + A_B).\n3. (b) Equal strain: strain = T/(A Y). Setting T_A/(A_A Y_A) = T_B/(A_B Y_B): (L − x)/(A_A Y_A) = x/(A_B Y_B), so x = L A_B Y_B/(A_A Y_A + A_B Y_B).\n4. Equal strain with equal lengths also means equal elongations, so the rod stays horizontal; equal stress does not guarantee a level rod unless the Y\u0027s are equal.\n5. (c) Statics gives T_A and T_B for any x (force and torque balance suffice), but whether the rod stays level depends on the wires\u0027 stretches Δl = T l/(A Y), an elastic (compatibility) condition. Equal elongations (equal strains, same length) keep the rod level, so it is level in case (b); in case (a) it is level only if Y_A = Y_B. With more supports than equations (a four-legged table) statics alone cannot fix the forces and the deformation must be included.\n6. Probes: A_A = A_B and Y_A = Y_B gives x = L/2 for both; a stiffer wire (larger Y A) must carry more of the load, so the weight must be hung closer to it.","answers":["Equal stress: x = L A_B/(A_A + A_B)","Equal strain: x = L A_B Y_B/(A_A Y_A + A_B Y_B)","Rod level only for equal strains (case b); in case (a) level only if Y_A = Y_B: elastic compatibility of the stretches, not statics, decides"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 11.91 (symbolic) + §11.3, §11.4"},{"id":"11.3","title":"A wire as a spring: static and sudden loading","q":"A vertical wire of unstretched length l₀, cross-sectional area A and Young\u0027s modulus Y supports a mass m. (a) Show that the wire behaves as a spring with force constant k = YA/l₀. (b) Find the static elongation, the stored elastic energy, and show that the energy per unit volume is ½ × stress × strain = ½Yε². (c) If the mass is instead attached with the wire unstretched and released suddenly, find the maximum elongation and maximum stress, and compare them with the static values. Why does this matter in design?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Y = (F/A)/(Δl/l₀) gives F = (YA/l₀)Δl, which is Hooke\u0027s law F = kΔl with k = YA/l₀. A stiffer, thicker, shorter wire is stiffer as a spring.\n2. (b) Static: Δl_s = mg/k = mg l₀/(YA). Energy U = ½kΔl² = ½(YA/l₀)Δl² = ½ (F/A)(Δl/l₀)(A l₀) = ½ × stress × strain × volume. So u = U/V = ½ Y ε² = σ²/2Y.\n3. (c) Released from the unstretched position, energy conservation (no damping): m g Δl_max = ½ k Δl_max², so Δl_max = 2mg/k = 2Δl_s. The maximum stress is 2 mg/A, twice the static stress; the mass oscillates between 0 and 2Δl_s about the static equilibrium.\n4. Design implication: a suddenly applied load doubles the peak stress (and with a dropped load, impact, much more), so safety factors for impact, cranes and lifts include dynamic factors. With friction or damping the amplitude decays toward the static value.\n5. Probes: check the dimensions of k (N/m), the energy density (Pa = J/m³) and the limit Y → ∞ (rigid wire, no energy stored).","answers":["k = YA/l₀","Δl_s = mg l₀/(YA); U = ½kΔl² = ½·stress·strain·volume; u = ½Yε²","Sudden loading: Δl_max = 2Δl_s and σ_max = 2mg/A (twice the static stress)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 11.88 (Hooke\u0027s law for a wire) + §11.4–11.5"},{"id":"11.4","title":"Block on an incline: tip or slip first?","q":"A uniform rectangular block of height h and base length b (measured along the incline) rests on a rough plane inclined at angle θ, with coefficient of static friction μ_s. The angle is increased slowly. (a) Find the angle at which the block is about to slip and the angle at which it is about to tip over. (b) Which occurs first, in terms of μ_s, b and h? (c) Explain using the center of gravity why a tall, narrow block tips and why a low, wide block slips, and what changes for a loaded truck.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Slipping: along the incline f_s = mg sin θ and normal force n = mg cos θ, with f_s ≤ μ_s n. Slip begins at mg sin θ = μ_s mg cos θ, i.e. tan θ_slip = μ_s.\n2. Tipping: the block is about to tip about its lower edge when the vertical line through the cg passes through that edge. The cg is at height h/2 above the incline and b/2 along it from the lower edge, so tan θ_tip = (b/2)/(h/2) = b/h. (Torque check about the lower edge: the weight\u0027s torque b/2 · mg cos θ − h/2 · mg sin θ changes sign at tan θ = b/h; at this point the normal force acts at the lower edge.)\n3. Which first? The smaller tangent: tipping occurs first if b/h \u003c μ_s (tall, narrow block); slipping occurs first if μ_s \u003c b/h (low, wide block or smooth surface).\n4. Center of gravity picture: a body is stable while the vertical line through its cg falls within the area of support; raising the cg (tall or top-heavy loads) or narrowing the base reduces the critical angle.\n5. A loaded truck has a higher cg, so b/h falls and it tips on a smaller slope (or in a turn, where tipping begins when the resultant of weight and inertial force passes through the outer wheel).\n6. Probes: for a cube b/h = 1, so tipping at 45° needs μ_s \u003e 1; a typical μ_s \u003c 1 means a cube slips first.","answers":["θ_slip = arctan μ_s","θ_tip = arctan(b/h)","Tips first if b/h \u003c μ_s; slips first if μ_s \u003c b/h","Stability condition: cg line inside the support base; a high load lowers the critical angle"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 11.81 (symbolic) + §11.2, Fig. 11.5"},{"id":"12.1","title":"Draining a tank through a small hole","q":"A large open cylindrical tank of diameter D contains water to height H. A small circular hole of diameter d (d much less than D) is opened in the bottom. Ignoring viscosity, (a) find the water height y(t) in terms of H, D, d, g and the time t after the hole is opened; (b) find the time T to empty the tank; (c) by what factor does T change if H is doubled? Also state what approximation you made about the speed of the surface and how you would correct for it.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: quasi-steady Bernoulli between the free surface and the jet, then continuity to turn the exit speed into dy/dt.\n2. Surface speed is tiny (v₁ = (d/D)² v₂ ≪ v₂), so Torricelli applies: $v_2=\\sqrt{2gy}$ (both ends at p_atm).\n3. Volume balance: $\\tfrac{\\pi D^2}{4}\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-\\tfrac{\\pi d^2}{4}\\sqrt{2gy}$, so $\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-\\left(\\dfrac{d}{D}\\right)^2\\sqrt{2g}\\,\\sqrt{y}$.\n4. Separate: $\\dfrac{d\\sqrt{y}}{dt}=-\\left(\\dfrac{d}{D}\\right)^2\\sqrt{g/2}$, hence $y(t)=\\left[\\sqrt{H}-\\left(\\dfrac{d}{D}\\right)^2\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{2}}\\,t\\right]^2$.\n5. Empty when the bracket vanishes: $T=\\dfrac{D^2}{d^2}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2H}{g}}$.\n6. T scales as √H, so doubling H multiplies T by √2 (the flow is slower at lower head, so it does not double).\n7. Probes: dy/dt \u003c 0 and its magnitude decreases as y falls (y(t) is a parabola in t); exact Bernoulli with v₁ ≠ 0 multiplies v₂ by 1/√(1 − (d/D)⁴), a correction of order (d/D)⁴; unsteady (∂v/∂t) terms are neglected because the level changes slowly.","answers":["y(t) = [√H − (d/D)²·√(g/2)·t]²","T = (D/d)²·√(2H/g)","Doubling H multiplies T by √2"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Bridging Problem, ch. 12 (How Long to Drain?)"},{"id":"12.2","title":"Balloons in an accelerating car","q":"A closed car (windows shut) accelerates forward on a level road with constant acceleration a, so the air inside, of uniform density ρ, accelerates with it and is at rest relative to the car. (a) Apply Newton\u0027s second law to a thin slab of air of thickness dx (along the direction of motion, x pointing backward from the windshield, area A) to find how the pressure varies along the car. (b) Show that the net pressure force on a balloon of volume V is ρVa, directed forward. (c) For a balloon of average density ρ_bal and negligible friction, find its acceleration relative to the car, and say which way a helium balloon and a dense ball move relative to the car and why.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the pressure gradient needed to accelerate the surrounding air also acts on any object immersed in it; it is the accelerated-frame analogue of buoyancy (∇p = −ρ(g − a) in general).\n2. (a) Slab: pressure p + dp on the rear face (larger x) pushes forward, p on the front face pushes backward. Newton: $(p+dp)A-pA=(\\rho A\\,dx)\\,a$, so $dp/dx=\\rho a$ and $p=p_0+\\rho a x$: pressure is higher toward the rear.\n3. (b) The pressure field does not care what fills the volume. Replace the air in the balloon\u0027s volume by the balloon: pressure force = the force that accelerated that air = $\\rho V a$, forward (rear pressure exceeds front).\n4. (c) Newton for the balloon (mass ρ_bal V): $a_{bal}=\\dfrac{\\rho V a}{\\rho_{bal}V}=\\dfrac{\\rho}{\\rho_{bal}}a$. In the car frame $a_{rel}=a_{bal}-a=\\left(\\dfrac{\\rho}{\\rho_{bal}}-1\\right)a$.\n5. Helium balloon (ρ_bal ≪ ρ): a_rel ≈ (ρ/ρ_bal)a forward, large, so it moves toward the windshield. A dense ball (ρ_bal \u003e ρ) has a_rel \u003c 0: it moves backward (inertia). A neutral object (ρ_bal = ρ) stays at rest relative to the car.\n6. Checks: ρ_bal → ρ gives a_rel = 0; vertically the same logic gives buoyancy with g replaced by g − a_vertical. Pressure variation over 2.5 m at a = 5 m/s² is about 15 Pa, so uniform ρ is fine.","answers":["dp/dx = ρa (pressure rises toward the rear)","Net pressure force on balloon = ρVa forward","a_rel = (ρ/ρ_bal − 1)·a; helium balloon moves forward, dense objects move backward"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 12.88"},{"id":"12.3","title":"Rock on a cord in an accelerating elevator","q":"A rock of mass m and density ρ_r hangs from the elevator ceiling by a light cord, completely immersed in a bucket of water (density ρ_w) that stands on the elevator floor; the rock touches neither bottom nor sides. (a) At rest, express the cord tension T₀ and use it to find the rock\u0027s volume. (b) Derive the tension T when the elevator accelerates upward with magnitude a. (c) Explain physically why the buoyant force changes, and find the tension when the elevator is in free fall.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: in the elevator frame the effective gravity is g_eff = g + a (upward acceleration); the water pressure gradient is ρ_w g_eff, so buoyancy scales with it.\n2. (a) Rest: $T_0=mg-\\rho_wVg=(m-\\rho_wV)g$, so $V=\\dfrac{mg-T_0}{\\rho_w g}$ (m/ρ_r = V). (For m = 3.00 kg, T₀ = 21.0 N this gives V = 8.57 × 10⁻⁴ m³.)\n3. (b) Newton on the rock, taking up as positive: $T+B-mg=ma$ with $B=\\rho_wV(g+a)$ (pressure gradient in the accelerating water, dp/dy = −ρ_w(g + a)).\n4. So $T=m(g+a)-\\rho_wV(g+a)=(m-\\rho_wV)(g+a)=T_0\\left(1+\\dfrac{a}{g}\\right)$, or $T=m(g+a)\\left(1-\\dfrac{\\rho_w}{\\rho_r}\\right)$.\n5. Numerically a = 2.50 m/s² gives T = 21.0 × (12.3/9.8) = 26.4 N.\n6. (c) The water must itself accelerate upward, so the pressure gradient increases to ρ_w(g + a); the displaced water\u0027s apparent weight, hence B, grows by the same factor as the rock\u0027s apparent weight. In free fall (a = −g) the gradient and B vanish, and T = 0: weightlessness.\n7. Probes: the factor (1 − ρ_w/ρ_r) is unchanged, so a rock denser than water always has T \u003e 0; if ρ_r = ρ_w, T = 0 at any a.","answers":["T₀ = (m − ρ_w V)g, V = (mg − T₀)/(ρ_w g)","T = (m − ρ_w V)(g + a) = T₀(1 + a/g)","Free fall (a = −g): T = 0 because the pressure gradient, and so buoyancy, disappears"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 12.96"},{"id":"12.4","title":"Dropping a heavy ball from a floating boat","q":"A boat of mass M floats in a swimming pool of horizontal area A (water density ρ_w). In the boat is a ball of mass m and density ρ_b \u003e ρ_w. (a) The ball is dropped over the side and sinks to the bottom: does the water level in the pool rise, fall or stay the same, and by how much? (b) How does the answer change if the ball\u0027s density is less than ρ_w so that it floats? (c) Would the level change when a floating ice cube melts in a glass of water, and why?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: compare the total volume of water displaced before and after; the level changes by ΔV_displaced/A.\n2. Before: boat plus ball float, so buoyancy supports both: displaced volume $V_1=\\dfrac{M+m}{\\rho_w}$.\n3. (a) After: the boat displaces $M/\\rho_w$, the sunken ball displaces its own volume $m/\\rho_b$. $V_2=\\dfrac{M}{\\rho_w}+\\dfrac{m}{\\rho_b}$.\n4. Change in displaced volume: $V_2-V_1=m\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\rho_b}-\\dfrac{1}{\\rho_w}\\right)\u003c0$, so the level falls by $\\Delta h=\\dfrac{m}{A}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\rho_w}-\\dfrac{1}{\\rho_b}\\right)$. Reason: afloat, the ball displaced its weight in water (volume larger than its own volume); sunk, only its own volume.\n5. (b) If ρ_b \u003c ρ_w the ball floats and still displaces m/ρ_w in total: V₂ = V₁, no change (this is the ice-cube case).\n6. (c) Ice floating displaces water of weight equal to its own; melting produces exactly that mass of water, with the same volume, so the level is unchanged (ignoring thermal expansion and dissolved material).\n7. Probes: limit ρ_b → ρ_w gives Δh → 0; ρ_b → ∞ gives the maximum fall m/(Aρ_w). A bird landing on the boat adds weight and the level rises by m/(ρ_w A).","answers":["Level falls: Δh = (m/A)(1/ρ_w − 1/ρ_b)","Floating ball (ρ_b \u003c ρ_w): no change; melting floating ice: no change","Reason: a floating object displaces its weight of water, a sunk one only its volume"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Discussion Questions Q12.20, Q12.21, Q12.23 + §12.3"},{"id":"13.1","title":"Satellite speeds up as air drag removes its energy","q":"A satellite of mass m is in a nearly circular orbit of radius r around a planet of mass M. Air drag slowly shrinks the radius by a small amount Δr ≪ r. (a) Show that the orbital speed increases by Δv = (Δr/2)√(GM/r³), and find the changes ΔK, ΔU and the work W_drag done by the drag force, to first order in Δr. (b) Resolve the apparent paradox that the satellite speeds up although drag does negative work. (c) What does this imply for where the lost energy goes?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Circular orbit: v = √(GM/r), K = GMm/2r, U = −GMm/r, E = −GMm/2r.\n2. Decrease r by Δr: dv = −½√(GM) r^{−3/2} dr gives Δv = +(Δr/2)√(GM/r³) \u003e 0.\n3. K = GMm/2r: ΔK = +GMmΔr/(2r²) (K increases).\n4. U = −GMm/r: ΔU = −GMmΔr/r² (U decreases by twice as much as K increases).\n5. Total energy change ΔE = ΔK + ΔU = −GMmΔr/(2r²) = W_drag (only non-conservative work): negative, as drag removes energy.\n6. Paradox: drag is not the only force that does work; gravity does positive work W_grav = −ΔU = +GMmΔr/r² as the satellite spirals inward (it has a component of force along the inward motion). Half of this goes into extra kinetic energy (ΔK = ½W_grav), and the other half is removed by drag (W_drag = −½W_grav). So speeding up is consistent with negative drag work.\n7. The energy lost ends as heat in the air and the satellite; ultimately a satellite that descends far enough burns up and the whole mechanical energy is dissipated.\n8. Probes: signs, factor-of-two between ΔK and ΔU (virial result K = −E = −U/2); small-Δr approximation.","answers":["Δv = (Δr/2)√(GM/r³) \u003e 0","ΔK = GMmΔr/(2r²), ΔU = −GMmΔr/r², W_drag = ΔE = −GMmΔr/(2r²)","Gravity does the positive work GMmΔr/r²; half becomes kinetic energy, half is dissipated by drag"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 13.70"},{"id":"13.2","title":"Two stars orbiting their common center of mass","q":"Two stars of masses m₁ and m₂ are a distance d apart and move in circular orbits about their common center of mass. (a) Show that the orbital radii satisfy r₁/r₂ = m₂/m₁, and explain why the two stars have the same period. (b) Show that the period is T = 2π√(d³/[G(m₁ + m₂)]) and find the speed of each star. (c) Show that this reduces to Kepler\u0027s third law for a planet when m₂ ≪ m₁, and say how astronomers use observed r and T to weigh the system.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The stars stay on opposite sides of the cm at all times, so r₁ + r₂ = d and m₁r₁ = m₂r₂ (definition of the cm), giving r₁ = m₂d/(m₁ + m₂), r₂ = m₁d/(m₁ + m₂), r₁/r₂ = m₂/m₁. The line joining them rotates rigidly, so both have the same angular speed and period T.\n2. Gravity gives the centripetal force on star 1: G m₁m₂/d² = m₁ ω² r₁ = m₁ ω² m₂ d/(m₁ + m₂).\n3. Cancel m₁m₂ d: G/d³ = ω²/(m₁ + m₂), so ω² = G(m₁ + m₂)/d³ and T = 2π√(d³/[G(m₁ + m₂)]).\n4. Speeds: v₁ = ωr₁ = m₂ ω d/(m₁ + m₂), v₂ = m₁ ω d/(m₁ + m₂); the lighter star moves faster.\n5. Limit m₂ ≪ m₁: m₁ + m₂ → m₁, r₁ → 0, so the heavy star is nearly at rest and T = 2π√(d³/G m₁), the Kepler result with a = d.\n6. Use: measuring d (or the radii) and T gives the total mass m₁ + m₂ = 4π²d³/(GT²), and the speed ratio or the radius ratio gives each mass separately; this is how stellar masses and extrasolar planets (from the star\u0027s wobble) are found.\n7. Probes: for equal masses r₁ = r₂ = d/2 and each star orbits at the speed √(GM/(2d)).","answers":["r₁/r₂ = m₂/m₁ with r₁ + r₂ = d; same angular speed hence same T","T = 2π√(d³/[G(m₁ + m₂)]); v₁ = m₂ωd/(m₁ + m₂), v₂ = m₁ωd/(m₁ + m₂)","m₂ ≪ m₁ gives Kepler\u0027s third law; m₁ + m₂ = 4π²d³/(GT²)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 13.72 (binary star)"},{"id":"13.3","title":"Perigee and apogee speeds of an elliptical orbit","q":"A spacecraft of mass m orbits a planet of mass M in an ellipse with perigee distance r_p and apogee distance r_a, both measured from the planet\u0027s center. (a) Use conservation of angular momentum to relate the speeds at perigee and apogee, and conservation of energy to find v_p and v_a in terms of G, M, r_p and r_a. (b) Show that the total energy is E = −GMm/(r_p + r_a) and find the period. (c) By how much must the speed at perigee increase to escape? (d) Check the circular limit r_a = r_p.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. At perigee and apogee the velocity is perpendicular to the radius, and gravity is a central force, so L = m r v is conserved: r_p v_p = r_a v_a, hence v_p/v_a = r_a/r_p (faster at the closer point; Kepler\u0027s second law).\n2. Energy: ½v_p² − GM/r_p = ½v_a² − GM/r_a. Substitute v_a = v_p r_p/r_a: ½v_p²(1 − r_p²/r_a²) = GM(1/r_p − 1/r_a) = GM(r_a − r_p)/(r_p r_a).\n3. So v_p² = 2GM r_a/[r_p(r_a + r_p)], v_p = √(2GM r_a/[r_p(r_a + r_p)]), and v_a = v_p r_p/r_a = √(2GM r_p/[r_a(r_a + r_p)]).\n4. Total energy E = ½m v_p² − GMm/r_p = GMm[r_a/(r_p(r_a + r_p)) − 1/r_p] = −GMm/(r_a + r_p) = −GMm/(2a) with a = (r_a + r_p)/2.\n5. Period (Kepler\u0027s third law): T = 2πa^{3/2}/√(GM) = 2π[(r_a + r_p)/2]^{3/2}/√(GM).\n6. Escape needs E = 0 at perigee: v_esc = √(2GM/r_p), so Δv = √(2GM/r_p) − v_p = √(2GM/r_p)[1 − √(r_a/(r_a + r_p))].\n7. Circular limit r_a = r_p = r: v = √(GM/r), E = −GMm/2r, T = 2πr^{3/2}/√(GM), Δv = (√2 − 1)√(GM/r). All reduce correctly.","answers":["v_p/v_a = r_a/r_p; v_p = √(2GMr_a/[r_p(r_a + r_p)]), v_a = √(2GMr_p/[r_a(r_a + r_p)])","E = −GMm/(r_a + r_p) = −GMm/2a; T = 2πa^{3/2}/√(GM)","Δv_escape = √(2GM/r_p) − v_p"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 13.77 (symbolic) + §13.5"},{"id":"13.4","title":"Falling through a tunnel in the earth and orbiting just above it","q":"Assume the earth is a uniform sphere of mass M and radius R, and a frictionless tunnel is bored through its center along a diameter. (a) Show that a mass dropped into the tunnel executes simple harmonic motion and find its period in terms of g and R. (b) Compare it with the period of a satellite in a circular orbit just above the surface and explain why they agree. (c) How would the force change if the density increased toward the center, as in the real earth?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Inside a uniform sphere only the mass within radius r attracts: M(r) = M r³/R³ (shells outside give zero net force). So F = −GM(r)m/r² = −(GMm/R³) r, a linear restoring force toward the center.\n2. With g = GM/R²: m d²r/dt² = −(mg/R) r, so ω² = g/R and T = 2π√(R/g), about 84 min for the earth.\n3. Surface-skimming circular orbit: v = √(GM/R) = √(gR), T = 2πR/v = 2π√(R/g): the same period.\n4. Why: the pouch\u0027s motion along a diameter is exactly the projection onto the diameter of a uniform circular motion of radius R with angular speed ω = √(g/R) (inside a uniform sphere the force is the same linear \u0027spring\u0027 as the x-component of the centripetal force). A projection of circular motion is SHM with the same period, independent of amplitude.\n5. Non-uniform earth: the denser core means the enclosed mass M(r) exceeds M r³/R³ for r \u003c R, so the force is larger than the uniform model near the center; g rises slightly just below the surface before decreasing, the motion is no longer perfectly harmonic, and the period is somewhat shorter (about 38 min one way vs 42).\n6. Probes: independence of the mass of the pouch; at r = R the force is GMm/R² and at r = 0 it is zero; the period does not depend on the amplitude; the same holds for a frictionless tunnel along any chord, since the force component along the chord is again linear in the distance from the chord\u0027s midpoint.","answers":["SHM with ω² = g/R, T = 2π√(R/g) ≈ 84 min","Same as the period 2πR/v of a surface-skimming satellite (SHM is the projection of circular motion)","With density increasing toward the center the force is larger inside and the motion is no longer exactly SHM"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 13.10 + §13.4, §13.6 (Chapter 14 preview)"},{"id":"14.1","title":"Damping regimes and energy loss per cycle","q":"A mass m on a spring of force constant k moves on a horizontal surface with a viscous drag force −bv. (a) Write the equation of motion and, by trying x = e^(st), find the conditions on b for oscillating, critically damped and overdamped motion; give ω′ for the oscillating case in terms of k, m, b. (b) Show that dE/dt = −bv² and use it to find the fractional energy lost per cycle for light damping, in terms of the quality factor Q = √(km)/b. (c) Explain why the fastest return to equilibrium without overshoot occurs at critical damping rather than at larger b.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Newton\u0027s law: $m\\ddot x=-kx-b\\dot x$, i.e. $\\ddot x+2\\gamma\\dot x+\\omega_0^2x=0$ with $\\gamma=b/2m$, $\\omega_0^2=k/m$.\n2. Try $x=e^{st}$: $s^2+2\\gamma s+\\omega_0^2=0$, so $s=-\\gamma\\pm\\sqrt{\\gamma^2-\\omega_0^2}$. If $\\gamma\u003c\\omega_0$ (b \u003c 2√(km)) the roots are complex: $x=Ae^{-\\gamma t}\\cos(\\omega\u0027 t+\\phi)$ with $\\omega\u0027=\\sqrt{k/m-b^2/4m^2}$. If $\\gamma=\\omega_0$ (b = 2√(km)) there is a double root and $x=(C_1+C_2t)e^{-\\gamma t}$. If $\\gamma\u003e\\omega_0$ both roots are real and negative (overdamped).\n3. (b) $E=\\tfrac12mv^2+\\tfrac12kx^2$, $dE/dt=v(m\\ddot x+kx)=-bv^2\\le0$.\n4. For light damping $E\\approx E_0e^{-(b/m)t}$ (amplitude ∝ e^(−bt/2m), energy ∝ amplitude²). Over one period $T\\approx2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$: $\\Delta E/E\\approx bT/m=2\\pi b/\\sqrt{km}=2\\pi/Q$.\n5. (c) For b \u003e 2√(km) the slow root $a_2=\\gamma-\\sqrt{\\gamma^2-\\omega_0^2}$ decreases as b grows (it tends to k/b), so the return is slower; below critical the system overshoots and rings. At critical damping the decay rate γ = ω₀ is the largest non-oscillating rate.\n6. Probes: check ω′ → ω₀ as b → 0; ω′ is lower than ω₀, so period increases with damping; amplitude falls to 1/e in 2m/b; Q ≈ number of radians of oscillation per e-fold of energy.","answers":["m·x″ + b·x′ + kx = 0; oscillation if b \u003c 2√(km), critical at b = 2√(km), overdamped if b \u003e 2√(km)","ω′ = √(k/m − b²/4m²)","dE/dt = −bv²; ΔE/E per cycle ≈ 2πb/√(km) = 2π/Q","Critical damping has the fastest non-oscillatory decay because the slow root of the overdamped case shrinks as b grows"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §14.7 (Eqs. 14.41–14.45), derivation extension"},{"id":"14.2","title":"Amplitude, phase and peak of a driven oscillator","q":"A mass m on a spring (constant k) with viscous damping constant b is driven by F = F_max cos(ω_d t). (a) Assuming a steady-state response x = A cos(ω_d t − δ), find A and tan δ in terms of F_max, m, k, b, ω_d. (b) Find the driving frequency at which A is maximum, the peak value, and the condition for a peak to exist at all. (c) Show that at ω_d = √(k/m) the velocity is exactly in phase with the force and find the average power delivered. (d) Explain physically why the peak height depends on damping and what limits the amplitude of a lightly damped system.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Use complex notation: $F=\\mathrm{Re}[F_{max}e^{i\\omega_dt}]$, $x=\\mathrm{Re}[Ce^{i\\omega_dt}]$. Then $(k-m\\omega_d^2+ib\\omega_d)C=F_{max}$.\n2. $A=|C|=\\dfrac{F_{max}}{\\sqrt{(k-m\\omega_d^2)^2+b^2\\omega_d^2}}$, $\\tan\\delta=\\dfrac{b\\omega_d}{k-m\\omega_d^2}$ (displacement lags the force; δ from 0 at low frequency through π/2 at ω₀ to π at high frequency).\n3. (b) Minimise the denominator $D=(k-m\\omega^2)^2+b^2\\omega^2$: $dD/d\\omega^2=0$ gives $\\omega_{peak}^2=\\omega_0^2-\\dfrac{b^2}{2m^2}$. A peak exists only if $b^2\u003c2km$. At the peak $k-m\\omega^2=b^2/2m$ and $D=b^2(\\omega_0^2-b^2/4m^2)=b^2\\omega\u0027^2$, so $A_{max}=\\dfrac{F_{max}}{b\\,\\omega\u0027}$.\n4. (c) At $\\omega_d=\\omega_0$: $k-m\\omega_d^2=0$, so $\\delta=\\pi/2$ and $x=A\\sin\\omega_dt$, $v=A\\omega_d\\cos\\omega_dt$, in phase with F; $A=F_{max}/(b\\omega_0)$. Average power $\\overline P=\\tfrac12F_{max}v_{max}=\\tfrac12F_{max}^2/b$, equal to the damping loss $\\tfrac12b\\omega_d^2A^2$; this is the maximum of $\\overline P(\\omega_d)$.\n5. (d) At resonance the spring and inertia forces cancel and only the drag opposes the drive, so A ∝ 1/b. Energy input per cycle must equal dissipation; with little damping the amplitude grows until losses balance (or until nonlinearity or failure intervenes). The transient takes about 2m/b to build.\n6. Probes: ω_d → 0 gives A → F_max/k; ω_d → ∞ gives A → F_max/(mω_d²); b → 0 gives the divergence at ω₀; Q = ω₀/(2γ) gives A_peak ≈ Q F_max/k and bandwidth ≈ ω₀/Q.","answers":["A = F_max/√[(k − mω_d²)² + b²ω_d²]; tan δ = bω_d/(k − mω_d²)","Peak at ω_d² = k/m − b²/2m² (exists only if b² \u003c 2km); A_max = F_max/(b√(k/m − b²/4m²))","At ω_d = √(k/m), v is in phase with F and P_avg = F_max²/(2b)","Peak height ∝ 1/b because only damping resists the drive at resonance; lower damping gives taller, narrower peaks"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §14.8 (Eq. 14.46, Fig. 14.28), derivation extension"},{"id":"14.3","title":"A rolling cylinder on a spring","q":"A uniform solid cylinder of mass M and radius R has a horizontal spring of force constant k attached to its axle (the other end of the spring is fixed). The cylinder is displaced a distance x from equilibrium and released, and it rolls without slipping on a horizontal surface. (a) Using energy, find the period of the oscillation of its center of mass in terms of M and k. (b) Find the magnitude of the friction force at displacement x and the largest amplitude for which rolling without slipping persists (static friction coefficient μ). (c) Compare with the period if the cylinder instead slid without friction, and with a thin hoop that rolls.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: rolling without slipping gives $v_{cm}=\\omega R$; static friction does no work, so mechanical energy is conserved.\n2. $E=\\tfrac12Mv^2+\\tfrac12I\\omega^2+\\tfrac12kx^2$ with $I=\\tfrac12MR^2$, $\\omega=v/R$: $E=\\tfrac34Mv^2+\\tfrac12kx^2$.\n3. Compare with $\\tfrac12m_{eff}v^2+\\tfrac12kx^2$: $m_{eff}=\\tfrac32M$, so $\\omega_{osc}=\\sqrt{2k/3M}$ and $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3M}{2k}}$.\n4. (b) $Ma=-kx+f$ and $fR=I\\alpha$ with the rolling constraint gives $f=-\\tfrac12Ma$ in magnitude; with $a=-(2k/3M)x$: $|f|=kx/3$. Rolling holds while $kx/3\\le\\mu Mg$, i.e. $A_{max}=3\\mu Mg/k$.\n5. (c) Frictionless sliding: $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{M/k}$, shorter by the factor $\\sqrt{2/3}$ (rotational KE is absent). Hoop: $E=Mv^2+\\tfrac12kx^2$, $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{2M/k}$ (longer).\n6. Probes: friction lengthens the period without dissipating energy because static friction does no work; the result is independent of R; the effective inertia $m_{eff}=M(1+I/MR^2)$.","answers":["T = 2π√(3M/2k)","|f| = kx/3; rolling persists for amplitude ≤ 3μMg/k","Sliding: T = 2π√(M/k) (shorter by √(2/3)); hoop: T = 2π√(2M/k)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Bridging Problem, ch. 14 (Oscillating and Rolling)"},{"id":"14.4","title":"Gravity, elevators and oscillators","q":"Explain, with formulas, how the period of (a) a mass on a vertical spring and (b) a simple pendulum of length L changes when the apparatus is moved to the Moon, placed in an elevator accelerating upward with acceleration a, or placed in a freely falling elevator. For the spring, state what gravity does do to the system. Then (c) explain why the period of a pendulum clock is nearly, but not exactly, independent of amplitude and say whether the period increases or decreases with amplitude, with a qualitative argument.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Vertical spring: with x measured from the equilibrium, the net force is −kx at any g (the static stretch mg/k absorbs weight), so $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$ is independent of g: unchanged on the Moon, in an accelerating elevator or in free fall (where the equilibrium stretch becomes zero but the mass still oscillates). Gravity only sets the equilibrium extension Δl = mg/k.\n2. (b) The pendulum restoring force is $mg_{eff}\\sin\\theta$, so $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g_{eff}}$. Moon: $g_{eff}=g_{Moon}\\approx g/6$, T larger by √6. Elevator accelerating upward: $g_{eff}=g+a$, $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{L/(g+a)}$ (shorter); downward with a \u003c g: $g-a$ (longer). Steady velocity: no change. Free fall (a = g) or a space station: $g_{eff}=0$, no restoring force and no oscillation (T → ∞).\n3. (c) Small-angle SHM has T independent of amplitude. At larger amplitude the restoring force is mg sinθ, which is weaker than mgθ, so the effective spring softens and the period increases (series: T ≈ T₀[1 + Θ²/16]); for Θ = 15° the increase is under 0.5%, and a clock keeps good time as the swing decays.\n4. Probes: the same qualitative argument holds for a physical pendulum (sinθ again); mass cancels in the pendulum; a pendulum fails as a standard because g varies with location, while the spring\u0027s period needs only m and k.","answers":["Spring: T = 2π√(m/k) unchanged in all cases (g only shifts the equilibrium)","Pendulum: T = 2π√(L/g_eff); Moon T ≈ √6 times larger; elevator up: g_eff = g + a; free fall: no oscillation","Period increases slightly with amplitude because sinθ \u003c θ weakens the restoring force"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Discussion Questions Q14.8–Q14.11, Q14.14 + §14.5"},{"id":"15.1","title":"Waves on a rotating rope","q":"A uniform rope of length L and mass m is held at one end and whirled in a horizontal circle with constant angular velocity ω; ignore gravity. (a) Find the tension F at a distance r from the held end. (b) Find the speed of small transverse waves at that point. (c) Find the time for a transverse pulse to travel from one end of the rope to the other. Say where the tension and the speed are largest and smallest, and why.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the tension at r must supply the centripetal force for all the rope beyond r; then use $v=\\sqrt{F/\\mu}$ locally and integrate $dt=dr/v$.\n2. (a) The portion beyond r has mass $m(L-r)/L$ and its center of mass is at $(L+r)/2$ from the axis. $F(r)=\\dfrac{m(L-r)}{L}\\,\\omega^2\\,\\dfrac{L+r}{2}=\\dfrac{m\\omega^2}{2L}\\,(L^2-r^2)$.\n3. Check by a small segment: $F(r)-F(r+dr)=\\tfrac{m}{L}dr\\,\\omega^2r$ so $dF/dr=-m\\omega^2r/L$, integrating with F(L) = 0 gives the same result.\n4. (b) $\\mu=m/L$, so $v(r)=\\sqrt{F/\\mu}=\\omega\\sqrt{\\dfrac{L^2-r^2}{2}}$.\n5. (c) $t=\\int_0^L\\dfrac{dr}{v}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{\\omega}\\int_0^L\\dfrac{dr}{\\sqrt{L^2-r^2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{\\omega}\\cdot\\dfrac\\pi2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{\\sqrt2\\,\\omega}$.\n6. Checks and probes: tension and speed are greatest at the held end (r = 0: $F=m\\omega^2L/2$, $v=\\omega L/\\sqrt2$) and zero at the free end; the speed falls to zero at the tip, yet the travel time is finite; the time is independent of L and m; dimensions of t are 1/ω. The wave speed varies with position, so a pulse speeds up toward the held end.","answers":["F(r) = mω²(L² − r²)/(2L)","v(r) = ω√((L² − r²)/2)","t = π/(√2 ω), independent of L and m"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Bridging Problem, ch. 15 (Waves on a Rotating Rope)"},{"id":"15.2","title":"Energy density in a travelling string wave","q":"A string of linear mass density μ under tension F carries a sinusoidal wave y = A cos(kx − ωt). (a) Write the kinetic energy per unit length u_k in terms of the transverse velocity. (b) For a short segment of unstretched length dx with small slope ∂y/∂x, show that its stretched length is approximately dx[1 + ½(∂y/∂x)²] and deduce the elastic potential energy per unit length u_p. (c) Evaluate u_k and u_p for the wave and show that they are equal at every x and t. (d) Show that the instantaneous power equals the total energy per unit length times the wave speed, and say why that is reasonable.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) A segment of mass $\\mu\\,dx$ has speed $\\partial y/\\partial t$: $u_k=\\tfrac12\\mu\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\\right)^2$.\n2. (b) Stretched length $ds=\\sqrt{dx^2+dy^2}=dx\\sqrt{1+(\\partial y/\\partial x)^2}\\approx dx\\left[1+\\tfrac12(\\partial y/\\partial x)^2\\right]$ for small slope. The tension (taken constant, F) does work $F(ds-dx)$, which is the stored energy: $u_p=\\tfrac12F\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\right)^2$.\n3. (c) $\\partial y/\\partial t=\\omega A\\sin(kx-\\omega t)$ and $\\partial y/\\partial x=-kA\\sin(kx-\\omega t)$, so $u_k=\\tfrac12\\mu\\omega^2A^2\\sin^2(kx-\\omega t)$ and $u_p=\\tfrac12Fk^2A^2\\sin^2(kx-\\omega t)$. Since $v^2=F/\\mu=\\omega^2/k^2$, $Fk^2=\\mu\\omega^2$ and $u_p=u_k$.\n4. Total $u=u_k+u_p=\\mu\\omega^2A^2\\sin^2(kx-\\omega t)$, average $\\tfrac12\\mu\\omega^2A^2$.\n5. (d) $uv=\\mu v\\omega^2A^2\\sin^2(kx-\\omega t)=\\sqrt{\\mu F}\\,\\omega^2A^2\\sin^2(kx-\\omega t)$, exactly $P(x,t)$ of Eq. (15.23). The energy in each unit length moves along with the wave at speed v, so the power is energy density times speed (like $I=\\rho_E v$).\n6. Probes: both energies are maximum where the displacement is zero (steepest slope, fastest particle) and zero at the crests, unlike a single SHM oscillator whose K and U are out of phase; this is because the wave carries energy past a point, so the energy does not stay in the segment. Average power ½√(μF)ω²A² follows.","answers":["u_k = ½μ(∂y/∂t)²; u_p = ½F(∂y/∂x)²","u_k = u_p = ½μω²A² sin²(kx − ωt) for the travelling wave","P = (u_k + u_p)·v = √(μF) ω²A² sin²(kx − ωt)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 15.85"},{"id":"15.3","title":"Is the sum of two standing waves a standing wave?","q":"A guitar string of length L (fixed at both ends) is plucked so that the motion is y = y₁ + y₂ with y₁ = C sin(ω₁t) sin(k₁x) and y₂ = C sin(ω₂t) sin(k₂x), where k₁ and k₂ are the fundamental and second-harmonic wave numbers and ω₁ = v k₁, ω₂ = v k₂. (a) Express k₁, k₂, ω₂ in terms of L, v, ω₁. (b) Find the nodes of y₁, of y₂ and of the sum. (c) Describe the motion of the point x = L/2 and of x = L/4. (d) Does y₁ + y₂ form a standing wave? Explain, and say what the period of the total motion is.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Fixed ends give $k_1=\\pi/L$, $k_2=2\\pi/L=2k_1$, so $\\omega_1=\\pi v/L$ and $\\omega_2=2\\omega_1$.\n2. (b) Nodes of y₁: $\\sin k_1x=0$ at x = 0 and L. Nodes of y₂: x = 0, L/2, L. For the sum, x = 0 and x = L are always nodes (both terms vanish). At x = L/2, $y=C\\sin\\omega_1t$ (y₂ vanishes there) so L/2 is not a node of the sum; the point is a node only at the instants when sin ω₁t = 0.\n3. (c) x = L/2: $y=C\\sin\\omega_1t$ (pure fundamental motion, amplitude C). x = L/4: $y_1=C\\sin\\omega_1t\\,(\\sqrt2/2)$ and $y_2=C\\sin2\\omega_1t$, a mixture of two frequencies, so the motion is periodic but not sinusoidal.\n4. (d) A standing wave has the product form f(x)·g(t) with fixed nodes; here the shape changes with time (for example the graph at t = 0, T/8, T/4, ... shows the pluck moving along the string), the sum is not of product form, and the interior node positions are not fixed. It is a superposition of two normal modes, valid by the principle of superposition. The total motion repeats with the fundamental period $T_1=2\\pi/\\omega_1=2L/v$.\n5. Probes: the harmonic content gives the timbre; energy is the sum of the two mode energies (the modes are orthogonal, no cross term); a pluck near the end excites many harmonics.","answers":["k₁ = π/L, k₂ = 2π/L, ω₁ = πv/L, ω₂ = 2ω₁","Both modes share nodes at x = 0 and L only; y₂ also has a node at L/2 which is not a node of the sum","The sum is not a standing wave (no product form, changing shape); it is a superposition of two normal modes with period 2L/v"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 15.79 + §15.8 (normal modes, superposition)"},{"id":"15.4","title":"Energy in a standing wave","q":"A string fixed at both ends carries the standing wave y = A_SW sin(kx) sin(ωt). Explain, using the power formula P = −F (∂y/∂x)(∂y/∂t) (F is the tension), why no net energy flows along the string. Then describe how the energy of the string is exchanged between kinetic and potential forms and show that the total energy of a length L = nλ/2 is constant.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Compute the derivatives: $\\partial y/\\partial x=kA_{SW}\\cos kx\\,\\sin\\omega t$ and $\\partial y/\\partial t=\\omega A_{SW}\\sin kx\\,\\cos\\omega t$.\n2. $P=-Fk\\omega A_{SW}^2\\,\\sin kx\\cos kx\\,\\sin\\omega t\\cos\\omega t=-\\tfrac14Fk\\omega A_{SW}^2\\,\\sin2kx\\,\\sin2\\omega t$.\n3. The time average over a period of sin 2ωt is zero at every x, so there is no net energy flow; at any instant the power is positive on one side of a node and negative on the other (energy sloshes between the loops). P is identically zero at nodes (no motion) and at antinodes (zero slope, no transverse force). Equivalently, the incident and reflected waves carry equal and opposite powers.\n4. Energy densities: $u_k=\\tfrac12\\mu\\omega^2A_{SW}^2\\sin^2kx\\cos^2\\omega t$ and $u_p=\\tfrac12Fk^2A_{SW}^2\\cos^2kx\\sin^2\\omega t$ with $Fk^2=\\mu\\omega^2$.\n5. Integrate over $0\\le x\\le L=n\\lambda/2$ (where ∫sin²kx dx = ∫cos²kx dx = L/2): $E_k=\\tfrac14\\mu\\omega^2A_{SW}^2L\\cos^2\\omega t$, $E_p=\\tfrac14\\mu\\omega^2A_{SW}^2L\\sin^2\\omega t$.\n6. $E=E_k+E_p=\\tfrac14\\mu\\omega^2A_{SW}^2L$, constant. The string is all kinetic when it passes through the straight configuration (ωt = 0) and all potential at maximum displacement (ωt = π/2), like a collection of SHM oscillators.\n7. Probes: a standing wave stores energy but does not transport it, so it is not an energy source for the sound that the string radiates; damping (or radiation) makes the amplitude decay slowly.","answers":["P = −¼ F k ω A_SW² sin 2kx sin 2ωt, whose time average is zero at every point (no net flow)","E_k = E cos²ωt, E_p = E sin²ωt for the length L = nλ/2","E = ¼ μ ω² A_SW² L, constant in time"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §15.5 + §15.7, Discussion Questions Q15.3, Q15.4"},{"id":"16.1","title":"Beats between a source and its own echo","q":"A source of sound of frequency f₀ moves with speed u toward a large, smooth, stationary wall in still air (speed of sound v \u003e u). It hears its own direct sound (f₀) and the sound reflected from the wall. (a) Derive the frequency f of the reflected sound as heard by the moving source, in terms of f₀, u and v. (b) Find the beat frequency and, from it, the speed u in terms of f_beat, f₀ and v; give the approximation for u much less than v. (c) What happens as u approaches v, and why is the result not symmetric in the source and listener velocities?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Two Doppler shifts. Use $f_L=\\dfrac{v+v_L}{v+v_S}f_S$ with velocities positive in the direction from listener to source.\n2. Stage 1: the wall is a stationary listener and the source moves toward it ($v_S=-u$): $f_w=\\dfrac{v}{v-u}f_0$.\n3. Stage 2: the wall acts as a stationary source of frequency f_w, and the original source is now a listener moving toward it ($v_L=+u$): $f=\\dfrac{v+u}{v}f_w=\\dfrac{v+u}{v-u}f_0$.\n4. (b) Beats between f and f₀: $f_{beat}=f-f_0=\\dfrac{2u}{v-u}f_0$, so $u=\\dfrac{v\\,f_{beat}}{2f_0+f_{beat}}\\approx\\dfrac{v\\,f_{beat}}{2f_0}$ for u ≪ v. (A bat emitting 1.70 kHz and hearing 10.0 Hz beats flies at about 1.0 m/s in air at 344 m/s.)\n5. (c) As u → v the reflected frequency diverges (the crests pile up in front of the source; beyond v there is a shock wave). The Doppler shifts for sound are referenced to the medium, so a moving source and a moving listener with the same relative speed give slightly different shifts (the difference is second order in u/v); to first order the echo shift is 2u f₀/v.\n6. Probes: the radar speed gun and fetal-heartbeat ultrasound use this mixing to read speed from a beat frequency; for light the relativistic formula depends only on relative velocity.","answers":["f = f₀ (v + u)/(v − u)","f_beat = 2u f₀/(v − u), so u = v f_beat/(2f₀ + f_beat) ≈ v f_beat/(2f₀)","f diverges as u → v (shock wave); sound shifts are referenced to the medium, not just to relative motion"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problems 16.72, 16.74 + §16.8 (Doppler effect, double shift)"},{"id":"16.2","title":"Why an open pipe and a stopped pipe sound different","q":"For a pipe of length L filled with a gas of speed of sound v: (a) Derive the allowed frequencies of an open pipe and of a stopped pipe (closed at one end) from the boundary conditions on displacement and pressure. (b) Explain why a stopped pipe of the same length sounds an octave lower and lacks even harmonics. (c) Express the fundamental frequency of the stopped pipe in terms of T, M and γ, and explain the effect on pitch of raising the temperature and of replacing air by helium. (d) Why do wind players blow into their instruments before performing?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Boundary conditions: an open end is a displacement antinode (and a pressure node, since the pressure stays atmospheric); a closed end is a displacement node (a pressure antinode). A node and the next antinode are λ/4 apart.\n2. (a) Open pipe: antinodes at both ends so $L=n\\lambda/2$, $f_n=\\dfrac{nv}{2L}$, $n=1,2,3,\\dots$ Stopped pipe: antinode at the open end and node at the closed end so $L=n\\lambda/4$ with n odd, $f_n=\\dfrac{nv}{4L}$, $n=1,3,5,\\dots$.\n3. (b) The stopped pipe\u0027s fundamental $v/4L$ is half that of the open pipe\u0027s $v/2L$ (one octave lower); only odd multiples of $v/4L$ fit, so even harmonics (2f₁, 4f₁, ...) are absent, which is part of why a clarinet sounds different from a flute.\n4. (c) $v=\\sqrt{\\gamma RT/M}$, so $f_1=\\dfrac{1}{4L}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\gamma RT}{M}}$. Pitch rises as √T (about 0.2% per kelvin). Helium has a smaller M (4.0 vs 28.8 g/mol) and larger γ (1.67 vs 1.40), so v is about 2.9 times larger and the pitch rises by that factor for the same pipe.\n5. (d) Blowing warms the air column to its operating temperature; since $v$ and hence pitch depend on T, the instrument would otherwise drift out of tune with others.\n6. Probes: end corrections make the effective length slightly longer, so real harmonics are not exact integer multiples; the pressure pattern has nodes where the displacement pattern has antinodes.","answers":["Open: f_n = nv/2L (n = 1, 2, 3, ...); stopped: f_n = nv/4L (n = 1, 3, 5, ...)","Stopped fundamental is half the open one (an octave lower) and only odd harmonics are present","f₁ = (1/4L)√(γRT/M): pitch ∝ √T and ∝ 1/√M (helium raises it by about 2.9×); warming up brings the air column to its operating temperature"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §16.4, Discussion Questions Q16.3, Q16.5, Q16.6"},{"id":"16.3","title":"Two coherent speakers: amplitude, intensity and decibels","q":"Two identical loudspeakers, driven in phase by one amplifier, each alone produce intensity I₀ at a point C. The waves reach C with a phase difference φ because of the path difference. (a) Find the pressure amplitude and intensity at C with both speakers, in terms of I₀ and φ, and the corresponding change in sound intensity level in decibels relative to one speaker. (b) Evaluate for φ = 0 and φ = π and explain where the energy goes at φ = π. (c) Why is it wrong to add the intensities of the two speakers directly, and what is the average over all φ?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: waves of equal amplitude and frequency add as pressure amplitudes (superposition), and intensity ∝ p_max².\n2. Each speaker alone: pressure amplitude $p_0$ and $I_0=p_0^2/(2\\rho v)$. The combined pressure is $p_0\\cos\\omega t+p_0\\cos(\\omega t+\\varphi)=2p_0\\cos(\\varphi/2)\\cos(\\omega t+\\varphi/2)$, so $p_{max}=2p_0\\left|\\cos\\dfrac\\varphi2\\right|$.\n3. Intensity: $I=\\dfrac{p_{max}^2}{2\\rho v}=4I_0\\cos^2\\dfrac{\\varphi}{2}$. Level change relative to one speaker: $\\Delta\\beta=(10\\ \\mathrm{dB})\\log\\left(4\\cos^2\\dfrac{\\varphi}{2}\\right)$.\n4. (b) φ = 0: I = 4I₀ and Δβ = +6.0 dB (not +3 dB); φ = π: I = 0 (silence; level of minus infinity). Energy is conserved globally: at the points of destructive interference the energy is missing, but in other directions the intensity exceeds 2I₀ (constructive interference), so the total radiated power is unchanged. In terms of the path difference, φ = 2πd/λ, so φ = π corresponds to d = λ/2.\n5. (c) Intensities add only for incoherent waves (random relative phase), giving 2I₀. Coherent waves add in amplitude. The average of 4I₀cos²(φ/2) over φ is 2I₀, equal to the incoherent sum, which shows that interference only redistributes the power.\n6. Probes: with unequal amplitudes the minimum is not zero, $p_{max}=|p_1-p_2|$ at φ = π; the same reasoning gives active noise cancellation.","answers":["p_max = 2p₀|cos(φ/2)|; I = 4I₀ cos²(φ/2)","Δβ = (10 dB) log[4cos²(φ/2)]: +6 dB at φ = 0, silence at φ = π","Intensities add only for incoherent sources; averaged over φ the coherent result is 2I₀, so interference redistributes energy without destroying it"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Bridging Problem, ch. 16 (Loudspeaker Interference) + §16.6"},{"id":"16.4","title":"Speed of a supersonic plane from the boom delay","q":"A plane flies at constant altitude h and constant speed v_S greater than the speed of sound v (the same at all altitudes). An observer on the ground hears the sonic boom a time T after the plane passes directly overhead. (a) Derive the plane\u0027s speed in terms of h, v and T. (b) What condition on T, h and v is necessary, and what does it say physically? (c) Explain why the boom is heard later rather than at the instant of overhead flyover, and what happens to the result as T approaches 0 and h/v.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the shock wave is a cone with half-angle α satisfying $\\sin\\alpha=v/v_S$. The boom is heard when the cone surface reaches the observer.\n2. At time T after flyover the plane is a horizontal distance $v_ST$ beyond the overhead point; the cone surface through the plane makes angle α with the flight line, so $\\tan\\alpha=\\dfrac{h}{v_ST}$.\n3. Use $\\sin^2\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\tan^2\\alpha}{1+\\tan^2\\alpha}=\\dfrac{h^2}{v_S^2T^2+h^2}$ and set it equal to $v^2/v_S^2$: $v_S^2h^2=v^2(v_S^2T^2+h^2)$.\n4. Solve: $v_S^2(h^2-v^2T^2)=v^2h^2$, so $v_S=\\dfrac{v\\,h}{\\sqrt{h^2-v^2T^2}}=\\dfrac{v}{\\sqrt{1-v^2T^2/h^2}}$.\n5. (b) Need $vT\u003ch$, i.e. $T\u003ch/v$: the delay must be shorter than the time sound takes to travel straight down from the overhead point; otherwise no supersonic plane is consistent with the data.\n6. (c) The cone trails the plane; the boom reaches the observer only when the cone surface sweeps over him, after the plane has moved on, not at the moment of the overhead passage. As T → 0 the speed tends to v (Mach 1: the cone opens to 90°); as T → h/v the speed tends to infinity (the cone collapses to a line).\n7. Probe: Example 16.19 (Mach 1.75, h = 8000 m, v = 320 m/s, T = 20.6 s) returns v_S = 560 m/s.","answers":["v_S = v h/√(h² − v²T²)","Requires T \u003c h/v; T → 0 gives v_S → v, T → h/v gives v_S → ∞","The boom arrives when the shock cone reaches the observer, after the plane has passed"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problem 16.82 + §16.9 (Shock waves)"},{"id":"17.1","title":"Conduction through spherical and cylindrical shells","q":"Steady-state conduction occurs radially through (a) a spherical shell with inner radius a and outer radius b, and (b) a long hollow cylinder of length L with inner radius a and outer radius b. In each case the inner surface is held at T₁ and the outer surface at T₂ (T₁ \u003e T₂) and the material has uniform thermal conductivity k. Derive the total heat current H and the temperature T(r) inside the wall. Then show that both results reduce to H = kA(T₁ − T₂)/L when the shell is very thin, and explain physically why dT/dr is not uniform here although it is for a flat slab.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Key idea: in steady state the same heat current H crosses every concentric surface, but the area A(r) grows with r, so the gradient must shrink with r.\n2. Sphere: $H=-k(4\\pi r^2)\\,dT/dr$. Separate: $H\\int_a^b dr/r^2=-4\\pi k\\int_{T_1}^{T_2}dT$, so $H\\left(\\tfrac1a-\\tfrac1b\\right)=4\\pi k(T_1-T_2)$ and $H=\\dfrac{4\\pi k\\,ab\\,(T_1-T_2)}{b-a}$.\n3. Sphere profile: integrate from a to r: $T(r)=T_1-(T_1-T_2)\\dfrac{b(r-a)}{r(b-a)}$. Check: T(a)=T₁, T(b)=T₂.\n4. Cylinder: $H=-k(2\\pi rL)\\,dT/dr$, so $H\\ln(b/a)=2\\pi kL(T_1-T_2)$, giving $H=\\dfrac{2\\pi kL(T_1-T_2)}{\\ln(b/a)}$ and $T(r)=T_1-(T_1-T_2)\\dfrac{\\ln(r/a)}{\\ln(b/a)}$.\n5. Thin-shell limit: let $b-a=\\ell\\ll a$. Sphere: $4\\pi ab\\to A$, $b-a=\\ell$, so $H\\to kA\\Delta T/\\ell$. Cylinder: $\\ln(b/a)\\approx \\ell/a$ and $2\\pi aL=A$, so $H\\to kA\\Delta T/\\ell$.\n6. Probe: |dT/dr| ∝ 1/r² (sphere) or 1/r (cylinder), steepest at the inner surface, because the same power spreads over a larger area. Extension: for a pipe with outer convection h, adding insulation can increase heat loss until r = k/h (critical radius).","answers":["Sphere: H = 4πk ab (T₁−T₂)/(b−a); T(r) = T₁ − (T₁−T₂) b(r−a)/[r(b−a)]","Cylinder: H = 2πkL (T₁−T₂)/ln(b/a); T(r) = T₁ − (T₁−T₂) ln(r/a)/ln(b/a)","Thin shell limit: H → kA(T₁−T₂)/ℓ with ℓ = b−a; gradient varies because area grows with r while H is constant"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 17.124"},{"id":"17.2","title":"Relaxation of a heated rod with fixed-temperature ends","q":"A uniform rod of length L, cross-sectional area A, thermal conductivity k, density ρ and specific heat c has its sides perfectly insulated and both ends held at 0°C. Initially the temperature is T(x) = T₀ sin(πx/L), with x measured from the left end. (a) Find the initial heat current at the ends and at the center, and explain the result at the center. (b) Using energy conservation for a thin slice, derive the equation for ∂T/∂t in terms of the thermal diffusivity D = k/(ρc), and find the initial rate of temperature change at the center. (c) Define a relaxation time and show that it scales as L²/D; why does the rate of cooling fall as time passes?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Heat current: $H=-kA\\,\\partial T/\\partial x=-kAT_0(\\pi/L)\\cos(\\pi x/L)$. At x = 0, |H| = πkAT₀/L directed out of the rod; at x = L the same magnitude out the other end. At the center cos = 0, so H = 0 (symmetry, zero gradient).\n2. Energy balance for a slice dx: net heat in = $H(x)-H(x+dx)=-\\partial H/\\partial x\\,dx=kA\\,\\partial^2T/\\partial x^2\\,dx$. This equals $\\rho cA\\,dx\\,\\partial T/\\partial t$. Hence $\\partial T/\\partial t=D\\,\\partial^2T/\\partial x^2$.\n3. For the initial profile $\\partial^2T/\\partial x^2=-(\\pi/L)^2T$, so $\\partial T/\\partial t=-D(\\pi/L)^2T$. At the center $T=T_0$: $\\dot T=-\\pi^2DT_0/L^2$.\n4. Because the shape is preserved, $T(x,t)=T_0\\sin(\\pi x/L)e^{-t/\\tau}$ with $\\tau=L^2/(\\pi^2D)$. This is the time the center would take to reach zero at the initial rate.\n5. Rate falls with time since the curvature (and thus the gradient driving the flow) shrinks as T falls: exponential decay.\n6. Probes: dimensions of D (m²/s); doubling L quadruples τ; for copper D ≈ 1.1×10⁻⁴ m²/s so L = 0.10 m gives τ ≈ 9 s.","answers":["Ends: |H| = πkAT₀/L outward; center: H = 0 (zero gradient by symmetry)","∂T/∂t = D ∂²T/∂x² with D = k/(ρc); center: dT/dt = −π²D T₀/L²","τ = L²/(π²D); T decays exponentially, so the cooling rate falls as the gradient shrinks"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 17.123"},{"id":"17.3","title":"One-layer greenhouse model of a planet","q":"A planet absorbs a mean solar power per unit area S_abs (averaged over its whole surface) and has no atmosphere. (a) Treating the surface as a blackbody, find its equilibrium temperature T_e. (b) Now add a thin atmospheric layer that is transparent to sunlight but absorbs all infrared radiation and re-emits it equally upward and downward as a blackbody at temperature T_a. Find T_a and the surface temperature T_s in terms of T_e. (c) Explain why the surface must get warmer, and say what real effects make the true warming differ from this model.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Energy balance for the bare planet: absorbed = emitted, $S_{abs}=\\sigma T_e^4$, so $T_e=(S_{abs}/\\sigma)^{1/4}$.\n2. (b) Whole system (top of atmosphere): only the layer\u0027s upward emission leaves, and it must equal the absorbed sunlight: $\\sigma T_a^4=S_{abs}=\\sigma T_e^4$, so $T_a=T_e$.\n3. Surface balance: sunlight plus downward IR from the layer equals surface emission: $\\sigma T_s^4=S_{abs}+\\sigma T_a^4=2\\sigma T_e^4$, so $T_s=2^{1/4}T_e\\approx1.19\\,T_e$.\n4. Physical reason: the layer sends half of its absorbed energy back down, so the surface must radiate more (T⁴) to restore balance; the total outgoing power to space is unchanged.\n5. Numbers check: T_e ≈ 255 K gives T_s ≈ 303 K, more than the observed 287 K (about 33 K above T_e).\n6. Probes: real atmosphere is not a perfect IR absorber (CO₂ absorbs only some wavelengths), heat also leaves the surface by convection and evaporation, and clouds change S_abs. Use kelvins in T⁴ laws.","answers":["T_e = (S_abs/σ)^{1/4}","T_a = T_e and T_s = 2^{1/4} T_e ≈ 1.19 T_e (≈ 303 K for T_e = 255 K)","Surface must warm because the layer returns IR downward while the outgoing power to space must still equal absorbed sunlight"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §17.7 (greenhouse effect), extended as an in-text derivation"},{"id":"17.4","title":"Radiation balanced by conduction in a cold rod","q":"A uniform rod of length L, cross-section A and thermal conductivity k has insulated sides. One end is in good contact with a solid at its melting point T_C (latent heat of fusion L_f). The other end is blackened and exposed to walls at temperature T_w ≫ T_C. Assume k is constant. (a) Write the steady-state condition that fixes the temperature T of the blackened end. (b) Solve for T − T_C to leading order, stating the assumption you use. (c) Find the rate at which mass melts. (d) How do T and the melting rate change if the rod radius is doubled? Explain.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Steady state: radiation absorbed at the blackened end minus radiation it emits equals conduction down the rod: $A\\sigma(T_w^4-T^4)=kA\\,\\dfrac{T-T_C}{L}$.\n2. Assume the end temperature is close to T_C so that $T^4\\ll T_w^4$ (emission negligible); then $\\sigma T_w^4=k(T-T_C)/L$ and $T-T_C\\approx\\dfrac{\\sigma T_w^4L}{k}$. Area cancels.\n3. Melting rate: all conducted heat enters the solid, $H=kA(T-T_C)/L=A\\sigma T_w^4$, so $\\dot m=\\dfrac{A\\sigma T_w^4}{L_f}$.\n4. Doubling the radius multiplies A by 4. T − T_C does not change (A cancels) but H and $\\dot m$ increase by 4.\n5. Self-consistency: check $T^4\\ll T_w^4$ afterwards. With copper at low T (k ≈ 1670 W/m·K), L = 0.20 m, T_w = 500 K, T_C = 13.84 K (hydrogen): T − T_C ≈ 0.42 K, consistent.\n6. Probes: increase of k with a good conductor, why the conduction path acts like a thermal resistance L/(kA) in series with a nearly fixed radiative source.","answers":["Aσ(T_w⁴ − T⁴) = kA(T − T_C)/L","T − T_C ≈ σT_w⁴L/k (valid if T⁴ ≪ T_w⁴)","Melting rate = Aσ T_w⁴ / L_f","Doubling the radius leaves T unchanged and quadruples the heat current and melting rate"],"src":"Bridging Problem, ch. 17 (steady-state heat flow: radiation and conduction)"},{"id":"18.1","title":"Atmospheric pressure with a linear temperature lapse","q":"In the troposphere the air temperature falls roughly linearly with height: T = T₀ − αy, where T₀ is the surface temperature and α is the lapse rate. Assume air is an ideal gas of molar mass M, g is constant, and the air is in hydrostatic equilibrium. (a) Derive p(y) in terms of p₀ (surface pressure), T₀, α, M, g, R and y. (b) Show that your result reduces to the isothermal result p = p₀e^(−Mgy/RT₀) as α → 0. (c) Explain physically whether, at a given height, the pressure should be higher or lower than in the isothermal atmosphere with the same T₀, and why.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Hydrostatic balance $dp/dy=-\\rho g$ and the ideal-gas density $\\rho=pM/(RT)$ give $\\dfrac{dp}{p}=-\\dfrac{Mg}{R}\\dfrac{dy}{T_0-\\alpha y}$.\n2. Integrate from 0 to y: $\\ln\\dfrac{p}{p_0}=\\dfrac{Mg}{R\\alpha}\\ln\\dfrac{T_0-\\alpha y}{T_0}$, so $p=p_0\\left(1-\\dfrac{\\alpha y}{T_0}\\right)^{Mg/(R\\alpha)}$.\n3. Limit: for small α, $\\ln(1-\\alpha y/T_0)\\approx-\\alpha y/T_0$, so $\\ln(p/p_0)\\to-Mgy/(RT_0)$, the isothermal result.\n4. Physical reasoning: colder air is denser at a given pressure, so the pressure drops faster with height. The pressure scale height $RT/(Mg)$ shrinks as T falls, so at a given y the pressure is lower than in the isothermal case.\n5. Check numerically: T₀ = 288 K, α = 0.006 K/m, y = 8863 m gives about 0.31 atm, below the 0.34 atm of the isothermal model with T = 273 K; either is a sensible Everest value.\n6. Probe: why constant g and ideal gas are good approximations up to ~10 km, and what changes above the tropopause (T roughly constant).","answers":["p = p₀ (1 − αy/T₀)^(Mg/(Rα))","As α → 0, p → p₀ e^(−Mgy/(RT₀))","Pressure is lower than the isothermal prediction at the same height, because the colder air is denser and the scale height shrinks"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 18.92"},{"id":"18.2","title":"Why the rms speed exceeds the average speed","q":"A collection of N gas molecules has speeds v₁, …, v_N. Define the average speed v_av = (Σv_i)/N and the rms speed v_rms = √[(Σv_i²)/N]. (a) Prove that v_rms ≥ v_av, with equality only if all the speeds are equal. (b) For a gas obeying the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution, v_av = √(8kT/πm) and v_rms = √(3kT/m); find their ratio. (c) Explain why the molecular kinetic-theory pressure and the equipartition energy involve v_rms rather than v_av.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Variance identity: $\\dfrac1N\\sum(v_i-v_{av})^2=\\dfrac1N\\sum v_i^2-2v_{av}\\dfrac1N\\sum v_i+v_{av}^2=(v^2)_{av}-v_{av}^2$.\n2. The left side is a mean of squares, so it is ≥ 0, with equality only when every $v_i=v_{av}$. Hence $(v^2)_{av}\\ge v_{av}^2$, so $v_{rms}\\ge v_{av}$.\n3. Two-particle check: $\\dfrac{v_1^2+v_2^2}{2}-\\left(\\dfrac{v_1+v_2}{2}\\right)^2=\\dfrac{(v_1-v_2)^2}{4}\\ge0$.\n4. Maxwell–Boltzmann: $\\dfrac{v_{rms}}{v_{av}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3kT/m}{8kT/\\pi m}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3\\pi}{8}}\\approx1.085$.\n5. Squaring weights fast molecules more. Pressure follows from the momentum transfer ($p=\\tfrac13 nm(v^2)_{av}$) and kinetic energy $\\tfrac12m(v^2)_{av}=\\tfrac32kT$, both quadratic in speed, so v_rms appears naturally.\n6. Probes: 500–900 m/s example gives 714 vs 700 m/s; ratio v_mp : v_av : v_rms = 1 : 1.13 : 1.22.","answers":["v_rms² − v_av² = mean of (v_i − v_av)² ≥ 0, so v_rms ≥ v_av (equal only if all speeds equal)","v_rms / v_av = √(3π/8) ≈ 1.085","Pressure and energy depend on v² (momentum flux and kinetic energy), whose mean is v_rms²"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 18.93 (and Example 18.7)"},{"id":"18.3","title":"Equipartition and the heat capacity of solids","q":"A harmonic oscillator of mass m, spring constant k_s and amplitude A has x = A cos(ωt). (a) Show that the time-averaged kinetic energy equals the time-averaged potential energy over a period. (b) Using equipartition (kT/2 per quadratic term per atom), explain why a monatomic solid modeled as 3D oscillators has C_V = 3R, and what C_V would be for a two-dimensional crystal whose atoms move only in the plane. (c) Explain why measured C_V falls below these values at low temperature, and why a monatomic ideal gas has only (3/2)R even though it has the same three translational degrees of freedom.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. $K=\\tfrac12mv^2=\\tfrac12m\\omega^2A^2\\sin^2\\omega t$ and $U=\\tfrac12k_sx^2=\\tfrac12m\\omega^2A^2\\cos^2\\omega t$ (using $\\omega^2=k_s/m$). Over a period $\\langle\\sin^2\\rangle=\\langle\\cos^2\\rangle=\\tfrac12$, so $\\langle K\\rangle=\\langle U\\rangle=\\tfrac14m\\omega^2A^2=E/2$.\n2. Each degree of freedom of an oscillator has a kinetic and a potential quadratic term, each worth kT/2. A 3D solid atom: 3 kinetic + 3 potential = 6 terms: $E=3kT$ per atom, $E=3nRT$, $C_V=3R\\approx24.9$ J/mol·K (Dulong–Petit).\n3. A planar crystal with in-plane motion has 2 kinetic + 2 potential terms: $E=2kT$ per atom and $C_V=2R\\approx16.6$ J/mol·K.\n4. At low T, kT is smaller than the smallest vibrational energy step, so oscillators freeze out in the ground state and C_V falls below 3R (or 2R); this needs quantum mechanics.\n5. A point-like ideal gas has only kinetic terms (no interaction potential), so f = 3 and $C_V=\\tfrac32R$.\n6. Probes: diamond (stiff bonds, high vibrational quantum) is below 3R at room temperature; why C_P − C_V matters for gases but little for solids.","answers":["⟨K⟩ = ⟨U⟩ = E/2 = mω²A²/4","Solid: 6 quadratic terms per atom gives C_V = 3R ≈ 24.9 J/mol·K; 2D in-plane crystal: C_V = 2R ≈ 16.6 J/mol·K","Low T: vibrational modes freeze out (quantization), so C_V is lower; an ideal gas has no potential-energy terms, so C_V = (3/2)R"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problems 18.80 and 18.81 with §18.4"},{"id":"18.4","title":"Checking the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution","q":"The Maxwell–Boltzmann speed distribution is f(v) = 4π (m/2πkT)^(3/2) v² e^(−mv²/2kT). Using the integrals ∫₀^∞ v² e^(−av²) dv = √π/(4a^(3/2)) and ∫₀^∞ v⁴ e^(−av²) dv = 3√π/(8a^(5/2)), with a = m/2kT: (a) show that f(v) is normalized; (b) compute (v²)_av and show that it agrees with the kinetic-theory/equipartition value; (c) find the most probable speed and explain why it is less than v_rms.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Write $f(v)=4\\pi(a/\\pi)^{3/2}v^2e^{-av^2}$ with $a=m/2kT$.\n2. Normalization: $\\int_0^\\infty f\\,dv=4\\pi(a/\\pi)^{3/2}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt\\pi}{4a^{3/2}}=\\dfrac{4\\pi\\,a^{3/2}\\sqrt\\pi}{4\\pi^{3/2}a^{3/2}}=1$, as required for a probability density.\n3. Mean square: $(v^2)_{av}=\\int_0^\\infty v^2f\\,dv=4\\pi(a/\\pi)^{3/2}\\dfrac{3\\sqrt\\pi}{8a^{5/2}}=\\dfrac{3}{2a}=\\dfrac{3kT}{m}$.\n4. So $\\tfrac12m(v^2)_{av}=\\tfrac32kT$ and $v_{rms}=\\sqrt{3kT/m}$, consistent with $pV=\\tfrac23K_{tr}$ and equipartition.\n5. Most probable speed: $d(v^2e^{-av^2})/dv=0$ gives $2v-2av^3=0$, so $v_{mp}=1/\\sqrt a=\\sqrt{2kT/m}$.\n6. Reasoning: $v_{mp}\u003cv_{av}\u003cv_{rms}$ because the distribution has a long tail to high speeds, which raise the moments of v more than they shift the peak. Probes: effect of doubling T (all speeds × √2, peak area unchanged), and the same distribution for each velocity component being Gaussian.","answers":["∫₀^∞ f(v) dv = 1","(v²)_av = 3kT/m, hence v_rms = √(3kT/m), consistent with ½m(v²)_av = (3/2)kT","v_mp = √(2kT/m) \u003c v_rms because of the long high-speed tail"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Problems 18.84–18.86 (in-text derivation of Eqs. 18.32–18.35)"},{"id":"19.1","title":"Oscillations of a gas-supported piston","q":"A vertical cylinder of radius r contains n moles of ideal gas under a frictionless piston of mass m. Outside air has pressure p₀, and the whole cylinder sits in a constant-temperature bath. In equilibrium the piston is a height h above the bottom. (a) Find the equilibrium gas pressure p. (b) The piston is displaced up a small distance y ≪ h and released; find the net force and the oscillation frequency in terms of p₀, r, m, g and h. (c) Is the motion simple harmonic for large displacements? (d) How would the frequency change if the gas responded adiabatically instead of isothermally (heat-capacity ratio γ)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Equilibrium: $pA=p_0A+mg$ with $A=\\pi r^2$, so $p=p_0+\\dfrac{mg}{\\pi r^2}$.\n2. Displaced by y: the isothermal gas obeys $p\u0027(h+y)=ph$, so $p\u0027=\\dfrac{ph}{h+y}$.\n3. Net upward force: $F=p\u0027A-p_0A-mg=A(p\u0027-p)=-\\dfrac{Ap\\,y}{h+y}\\approx-\\dfrac{Ap}{h}\\,y$ for $y\\ll h$.\n4. This is a restoring force $F=-ky$ with $k=Ap/h$, so $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{Ap}{mh}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{p_0\\pi r^2+mg}{mh}}$ and $f=\\omega/2\\pi$.\n5. For large y the force $-Apy/(h+y)$ is not linear in y (it also differs for upward and downward displacements), so the motion is not exactly SHM; amplitude-dependent period.\n6. Adiabatic: $p\u0027=p\\,(h/(h+y))^{\\gamma}$ and $F\\approx-\\gamma(Ap/h)y$, so $\\omega_{ad}=\\sqrt{\\gamma}\\,\\omega_{iso}$ (stiffer spring). Probe: limit $p_0\\to0$ gives $\\omega=\\sqrt{g/h}$, and the link to the speed of sound.","answers":["p = p₀ + mg/(πr²)","F ≈ −(pπr²/h) y, ω = √[(p₀πr² + mg)/(m h)], f = ω/2π","Not SHM for large displacements (force ∝ y/(h+y) is nonlinear)","Adiabatic: ω is larger by √γ"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 19.69"},{"id":"19.2","title":"Adiabatic law and work from the first law","q":"For n moles of an ideal gas (heat capacity ratio γ, C_V constant) undergoing a quasi-static adiabatic process: (a) starting from the first law, derive pV^γ = constant; (b) derive the work done in going from (p₁, V₁) to (p₂, V₂) in terms of p, V and γ; (c) show that an adiabat through a point on a pV-diagram is steeper than the isotherm through the same point, and use this to compare the work done in an adiabatic and an isothermal expansion between the same initial state and the same final volume; (d) explain why a free expansion does not obey pV^γ = constant.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Q = 0 so $dU=-dW$: $nC_V\\,dT=-p\\,dV$ with $p=nRT/V$ gives $\\dfrac{dT}{T}=-\\dfrac{R}{C_V}\\dfrac{dV}{V}=-(\\gamma-1)\\dfrac{dV}{V}$ since $R=C_p-C_V$.\n2. Integrate: $TV^{\\gamma-1}=$ const. Replace T = pV/nR: $pV^{\\gamma}=$ const.\n3. Work: $W=\\int p\\,dV=\\int p_1V_1^\\gamma V^{-\\gamma}dV=\\dfrac{p_1V_1-p_2V_2}{\\gamma-1}$. Equivalent: $W=nC_V(T_1-T_2)$ (from ΔU = −W).\n4. Slopes: adiabat $dp/dV=-\\gamma p/V$, isotherm $dp/dV=-p/V$, so the adiabat is steeper by the factor γ \u003e 1.\n5. For an expansion to the same $V_2$, the adiabatic curve lies below the isothermal one (pressure falls faster since T drops), so the area, the work, is smaller: $W_{ad}\u003cW_{iso}$. Equivalent statement: the final pressure is lower and the gas cools.\n6. Free expansion is not quasi-static: the gas is not in equilibrium during the process, so $p$ is not defined along a path, and no work is done (W = 0) although V changes; for an ideal gas ΔT = 0 there. Probes: sign of ΔU for adiabatic compression and the limit γ → 1.","answers":["pV^γ = constant, equivalent to TV^(γ−1) = constant","W = (p₁V₁ − p₂V₂)/(γ − 1) = nC_V(T₁ − T₂)","Adiabat slope = γ × isotherm slope, so adiabatic expansion does less work than isothermal between the same initial state and final volume","Free expansion is irreversible (non-equilibrium, W = 0), so the quasi-static adiabat does not apply"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §19.8 (in-text derivation of Eqs. 19.21-19.26)"},{"id":"19.3","title":"Free expansion versus isothermal expansion","q":"An ideal gas at temperature T expands from volume V₁ to V₂ in two ways: (A) slowly and isothermally against a piston, with heat supplied from a reservoir; (B) as a free expansion into an evacuated, insulated, rigid compartment. (a) Find Q, W and ΔU for each path and compare the end states. (b) What does the free-expansion experiment tell us about how the internal energy of an ideal gas depends on p, V and T? (c) Suppose a real gas has U = nC_VT − an²/V (a \u003e 0). Find its temperature change in a free expansion and explain the sign physically.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (A) Isothermal ideal gas: $\\Delta U=0$ so $Q=W=nRT\\ln(V_2/V_1)$ (positive). (B) Free expansion: no heat (insulated) and no work (rigid walls), so Q = W = 0 and ΔU = 0.\n2. Both paths end at the same state (same T and V₂; hence the same p₂): ΔU is path independent while Q and W differ. This shows Q and W are not state functions.\n3. In (B) U is constant. Experiment shows low-density gases do not change T, so with U constant while p and V change, U cannot depend on p or V separately: for an ideal gas U = U(T) only, ΔU = nC_VΔT.\n4. Real gas: U is constant, so $nC_VT_1-an^2/V_1=nC_VT_2-an^2/V_2$ and $\\Delta T=T_2-T_1=-\\dfrac{an}{C_V}\\left(\\dfrac1{V_1}-\\dfrac1{V_2}\\right)\u003c0$.\n5. Physical meaning: attractive forces store potential energy that rises as the molecules move apart, so the kinetic energy (temperature) must fall; the ideal limit a → 0 gives ΔT = 0.\n6. Probes: sign of S change (preview of chapter 20), and the effect of finite size (b term).","answers":["Isothermal: Q = W = nRT ln(V₂/V₁), ΔU = 0; free expansion: Q = W = 0, ΔU = 0; same end state","For an ideal gas U depends only on T","Real gas: ΔT = −(an/C_V)(1/V₁ − 1/V₂) \u003c 0, a cooling from the potential energy of attraction"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §19.3 and §19.6 (free expansion, Figs. 19.8 and 19.17)"},{"id":"19.4","title":"Heat and work in a rectangular gas cycle","q":"n moles of a monatomic ideal gas go around the cycle 1→2→3→4→1 on a pV-diagram: 1 (p₁, V₁) → 2 (p₂, V₁) at constant volume; 2 → 3 (p₂, V₂) at constant pressure; 3 → 4 (p₁, V₂) at constant volume; 4 → 1 at constant pressure, with p₂ \u003e p₁ and V₂ \u003e V₁. Find in terms of p₁, p₂, V₁, V₂: (a) the net work in one cycle; (b) the heat exchanged in each leg and the net heat; (c) the heat absorbed in total (the positive legs) and show that Q_net = W_net; explain why ΔU = 0 over the cycle and why the sense of traversal matters.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Net work = area enclosed (clockwise, so positive): $W=(p_2-p_1)(V_2-V_1)$. Legs 1→2 and 3→4 do no work; $W_{23}=p_2(V_2-V_1)$, $W_{41}=-p_1(V_2-V_1)$.\n2. Monatomic: $C_V=\\tfrac32R$, $C_p=\\tfrac52R$, and $nR\\,\\Delta T=\\Delta(pV)$.\n3. $Q_{12}=nC_V\\Delta T=\\tfrac32V_1(p_2-p_1)$ (in); $Q_{23}=nC_p\\Delta T=\\tfrac52p_2(V_2-V_1)$ (in); $Q_{34}=-\\tfrac32V_2(p_2-p_1)$ (out); $Q_{41}=-\\tfrac52p_1(V_2-V_1)$ (out).\n4. Net: $Q_{12}+Q_{34}=-\\tfrac32(p_2-p_1)(V_2-V_1)$ and $Q_{23}+Q_{41}=\\tfrac52(p_2-p_1)(V_2-V_1)$, so $Q_{net}=(p_2-p_1)(V_2-V_1)=W_{net}$.\n5. Heat absorbed: $Q_{in}=Q_{12}+Q_{23}=\\tfrac32V_1(p_2-p_1)+\\tfrac52p_2(V_2-V_1)$.\n6. U is a state function, so ΔU = 0 around a cycle and Q = W. Reversing the sense (counterclockwise) flips the sign of both. Probes: the ratio W/Q_in is below 1 and foreshadows engine efficiency in the next chapter.","answers":["W_net = (p₂ − p₁)(V₂ − V₁) (clockwise)","Q₁₂ = (3/2)V₁(p₂−p₁), Q₂₃ = (5/2)p₂(V₂−V₁), Q₃₄ = −(3/2)V₂(p₂−p₁), Q₄₁ = −(5/2)p₁(V₂−V₁)","Q_net = W_net; Q_in = (3/2)V₁(p₂−p₁) + (5/2)p₂(V₂−V₁)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §19.4 (cyclic processes) and Examples 19.3-19.4"},{"id":"20.1","title":"Efficiency of the ideal Diesel cycle","q":"The ideal Diesel cycle for n moles of an ideal gas (heat-capacity ratio γ) is: a→b adiabatic compression from volume rV to V (compression ratio r); b→c heating at constant pressure to volume βV (β \u003e 1 is the cutoff ratio); c→d adiabatic expansion to rV; d→a cooling at constant volume. (a) Derive the thermal efficiency in terms of r, β and γ. (b) Show that it reduces to the Otto efficiency in the limit β → 1. (c) Explain why, for the same compression ratio, the Diesel cycle is less efficient than the Otto cycle, and why a Diesel engine can nevertheless be more efficient in practice.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Heat flows: $Q_H=nC_p(T_c-T_b)$ (constant pressure leg), $|Q_C|=nC_V(T_d-T_a)$ (constant volume leg). Hence $e=1-\\dfrac{|Q_C|}{Q_H}=1-\\dfrac{1}{\\gamma}\\dfrac{T_d-T_a}{T_c-T_b}$.\n2. Adiabat ab: $T_b=T_a\\,r^{\\gamma-1}$. Isobar bc: $T_c/T_b=V_c/V_b=\\beta$, so $T_c=\\beta T_b$.\n3. Adiabat cd: $T_d=T_c\\left(\\dfrac{\\beta V}{rV}\\right)^{\\gamma-1}=\\beta T_b\\,\\beta^{\\gamma-1}r^{1-\\gamma}=\\beta^{\\gamma}T_a$.\n4. Substitute: $e=1-\\dfrac{1}{\\gamma}\\dfrac{T_a(\\beta^\\gamma-1)}{T_ar^{\\gamma-1}(\\beta-1)}=1-\\dfrac{1}{r^{\\gamma-1}}\\,\\dfrac{\\beta^{\\gamma}-1}{\\gamma(\\beta-1)}$.\n5. Limit β → 1: $(\\beta^\\gamma-1)/(\\beta-1)\\to\\gamma$, so $e\\to1-1/r^{\\gamma-1}$, the Otto result.\n6. For β \u003e 1 the bracket $\\dfrac{\\beta^\\gamma-1}{\\gamma(\\beta-1)}\u003e1$, so the Diesel efficiency is lower at equal r. But Diesel engines compress air only (no pre-ignition), so r = 15–20 is possible versus 8–13, giving higher e overall.","answers":["e = 1 − [1/r^(γ−1)] · (β^γ − 1)/[γ(β − 1)]","β → 1 gives the Otto efficiency 1 − 1/r^(γ−1)","At equal r the Diesel is less efficient (bracket \u003e 1), but much larger r is allowed since there is no pre-ignition"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Challenge Problem 20.64 (symbolic), with §20.3"},{"id":"20.2","title":"Upper bound on efficiency from entropy","q":"A heat engine operates in a cycle between a hot reservoir at T_H and a cold reservoir at T_C, absorbing |Q_H| and rejecting |Q_C| per cycle. (a) Using the entropy form of the second law, prove that e ≤ 1 − T_C/T_H, and state when equality holds. (b) Show that the coefficient of performance of any refrigerator between the same reservoirs satisfies K ≤ T_C/(T_H − T_C). (c) An inventor proposes to beat the Carnot limit by running one Carnot engine between T_H and an intermediate temperature T_I and a second Carnot engine between T_I and T_C using the heat rejected by the first. Show that the composite engine has exactly the same efficiency as one Carnot engine between T_H and T_C.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Over a cycle the working substance returns to its initial state, so ΔS_engine = 0. The reservoirs change: $\\Delta S_H=-|Q_H|/T_H$, $\\Delta S_C=+|Q_C|/T_C$.\n2. Second law: $\\Delta S_{total}=-\\dfrac{|Q_H|}{T_H}+\\dfrac{|Q_C|}{T_C}\\ge0\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{|Q_C|}{|Q_H|}\\ge\\dfrac{T_C}{T_H}$.\n3. Hence $e=1-\\dfrac{|Q_C|}{|Q_H|}\\le1-\\dfrac{T_C}{T_H}$, with equality only if the cycle is reversible (ΔS_total = 0), i.e. a Carnot engine.\n4. Refrigerator: $|Q_H|=|Q_C|+|W|$ and $\\Delta S_{total}=\\dfrac{|Q_H|}{T_H}-\\dfrac{|Q_C|}{T_C}\\ge0$. Then $\\dfrac{|Q_C|+|W|}{T_H}\\ge\\dfrac{|Q_C|}{T_C}$, so $K=\\dfrac{|Q_C|}{|W|}\\le\\dfrac{T_C}{T_H-T_C}$.\n5. Composite: engine 1 has $|Q_I|=|Q_H|T_I/T_H$; engine 2 has $|Q_C|=|Q_I|T_C/T_I=|Q_H|T_C/T_H$. Total work $W=|Q_H|-|Q_C|$, so $e=1-T_C/T_H$, same as one Carnot engine.\n6. Probes: efficiency in terms of kelvins, why using the intermediate temperature gains nothing (T_I cancels), and why the Kelvin scale is defined from |Q_C|/|Q_H|.","answers":["e ≤ 1 − T_C/T_H (equality only for reversible engines) since ΔS_total = |Q_C|/T_C − |Q_H|/T_H ≥ 0","K ≤ T_C/(T_H − T_C)","Composite of two Carnot engines: |Q_C| = |Q_H|T_C/T_H, so e = 1 − T_C/T_H; no gain"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman §20.6-20.7 and Problem 20.53"},{"id":"20.3","title":"Entropy of equalization and the work thrown away","q":"Two identical bodies each have constant heat capacity C (in J/K) and initial temperatures T₁ and T₂ (T₁ \u003e T₂). (a) They are placed in thermal contact in an isolated enclosure. Find the final temperature and the total entropy change, and prove the entropy change is positive. (b) Instead, a reversible heat engine is run between them until they reach a common temperature T_f. Find T_f and the maximum work obtainable. (c) Interpret the difference between (a) and (b).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Energy conservation: $C(T_f-T_1)+C(T_f-T_2)=0\\Rightarrow T_f=\\dfrac{T_1+T_2}{2}$.\n2. Entropy of each body: $\\Delta S=C\\ln(T_f/T_i)$ (compute along reversible paths since S is a state function). Total $\\Delta S=C\\ln\\dfrac{T_f^2}{T_1T_2}=C\\ln\\dfrac{(T_1+T_2)^2}{4T_1T_2}$.\n3. Since $(T_1+T_2)^2-4T_1T_2=(T_1-T_2)^2\u003e0$, the argument exceeds 1 and ΔS \u003e 0 (equality only if T₁ = T₂): irreversible heat flow.\n4. (b) A reversible engine has ΔS_total = 0: $C\\ln\\dfrac{T_f}{T_1}+C\\ln\\dfrac{T_f}{T_2}=0\\Rightarrow T_f=\\sqrt{T_1T_2}$ (lower than the arithmetic mean).\n5. Work: $W=Q_{from\\ 1}-Q_{to\\ 2}=C(T_1-T_f)-C(T_f-T_2)=C(T_1+T_2-2\\sqrt{T_1T_2})=C(\\sqrt{T_1}-\\sqrt{T_2})^2$.\n6. Interpretation: direct contact wastes this work (the energy is conserved but is no longer available); entropy production measures the lost opportunity. Probes: limits T₁ → T₂ (W → 0) and T₂ → 0 (W → CT₁).","answers":["(a) T_f = (T₁+T₂)/2 and ΔS = C ln[(T₁+T₂)²/(4T₁T₂)] \u003e 0","(b) T_f = √(T₁T₂) and W_max = C(√T₁ − √T₂)²","(c) Mixing irreversibly gives W = 0 with entropy production; the maximum work is lost"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 20.10 and §20.7 (generalized in symbols)"},{"id":"20.4","title":"Free expansion by counting microstates","q":"N molecules of an ideal gas initially occupy volume V₁ of an insulated container; a partition is removed so the gas can occupy volume V₂ \u003e V₁. (a) Argue that the number of microscopic states changes by w₂/w₁ = (V₂/V₁)^N, and find ΔS from S = k ln w. (b) Show your result agrees with the thermodynamic value ΔS = nR ln(V₂/V₁) obtained along a reversible isothermal path. (c) Find the probability that a later fluctuation puts all N molecules back in V₁, and relate it to ΔS. (d) Evaluate qualitatively for N = 100 versus N = 10²⁴.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Velocities are unaffected by the free expansion (no work, no heat); each molecule\u0027s range of positions grows from V₁ to V₂. For independent molecules $w\\propto V^N$, so $w_2/w_1=(V_2/V_1)^N$.\n2. $\\Delta S=k\\ln\\dfrac{w_2}{w_1}=Nk\\ln\\dfrac{V_2}{V_1}$.\n3. (b) Use $Nk=nN_Ak=nR$: $\\Delta S=nR\\ln(V_2/V_1)$. The thermodynamic path (isothermal expansion, $Q=W=nRT\\ln(V_2/V_1)$, $\\Delta S=Q/T$) gives the same value, though the real process had Q = 0.\n4. (c) The probability that all N molecules are found in V₁ is $(V_1/V_2)^N=w_1/w_2=e^{-\\Delta S/k}$.\n5. (d) For V₂ = 2V₁: P = 2⁻ᴺ. For N = 100 that is about 10⁻³⁰, tiny; for N = 10²⁴ the exponent is about 3×10²³ digits, so effectively impossible.\n6. Conclusion: the second law is statistical; spontaneous entropy decreases are not forbidden in principle but have vanishing probability for macroscopic systems. Probe: why ΔS depends only on the end states.","answers":["w₂/w₁ = (V₂/V₁)^N, ΔS = Nk ln(V₂/V₁)","ΔS = nR ln(V₂/V₁), the same as the reversible isothermal path","P(all back in V₁) = (V₁/V₂)^N = e^(−ΔS/k): about 10⁻³⁰ for N = 100 and utterly negligible for N ~ 10²⁴"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Examples 20.8 and 20.11 with §20.8 discussion"}];
